{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters","id":136859,"title":"What's YOUR Custom Shifters?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-14T10:25:51-07:00","id":136859,"title":"What's YOUR Custom Shifters?","content":"Okay, so there are books which explain how to create other types of shapeshifters besides Werewolves. Out of curiosity, I'd like to know what (if any) types of other shapeshifters some of the more experienced players have come up with.<br /> <br /> Not having access to War Against the Pure, I have not officially wrote up anything yet. but I'm personally interested in creating Giraffes, Frogs, Badgers, Moles, Seals, Walruses, and Skunks.<br /> <br /> <br /> And come on, who doesn't want to see<b> Honey Badger</b> in Gauru form taking on a Predator King?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 05-14-2014, 01:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-14T11:43:58-07:00","id":136927,"title":"","content":"People cursed to be snakes because they somehow violated the Karmic Wheel in a major way and are now tightly bound by the laws of Karma and Destiny; and ravens, who didn't die when they should, and now owe a huge debt to the dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-14T13:24:56-07:00","id":137076,"title":"","content":"Hmm, judging by your descriptions of them, it sounds like its possible for ordinary people to become shapeshifters instead of having to inherit it. If that's true, it gives me a great idea for a series of shifters who are based on the character Vices! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-14T22:23:57-07:00","id":137734,"title":"","content":"It's the World of Darkness. Anything is possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-15T03:22:35-07:00","id":137949,"title":"","content":"Alright, here's the idea I came up with. Not sure if its passable, but it could be considered a rough draft for what I had in mind.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Beasts of Vice:</b> In cultures which are less about honoring animals and more about fearing them, being a shapeshifter is considered more of a curse than a gift. The Beasts of Vice are not natural shapeshifters, it was not passed onto them through heredity. Instead, they were once ordinary people who were transformed into shifters by powerful spirits who represent one of the 7 Vices. Sometimes this change is willing, with spiritually enlightened humans forming a pact with dark forces they don't fully comprehend. However, it is also possible to force the change upon a mortal who has proven themselves incredibly devoted (although unwittingly) to the spirit in question.<br /> <br /> <br /> Regardless of how it happened, the Beasts of Vice are now bound to these malevolent spirits who take on the role of being their totem. These shifters are tasked with going out and fostering more dark spirits who their totem can then consume, dominate, or merge with in order to increase their own powers. This tactic has two different strategies to it. Either it draws attention away from any Bale Hounds (who are generally far more experienced at this line of work) in the area, or the Werewolves believe it is actually the work of a Bale Hound and turn against each other trying to find the traitor.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Beasts of Vice have no auspices in the traditional sense. Instead, their natures are derived from which of the 7 vices they represent. Lust, Sloth, Envy, Greed, Wrath, Gluttony, or Pride. Their Vice dictates the Totem they are meant to serve, and thus provides the guidance to their path in the spirit world as opposed it some preordained destiny.<br /> <br /> <br /> The accounts of how the first Beasts of Vice were created is sketchy and based on hearsay. Some claim that the spirits were desperate for a way to retaliate against the Werewolves who are gaining dominance over the territory. Others claim it was an act of spite, a way to insult both the Uratha and their cultural heritage. In truth, it is actually both, as the darker spirits came to realize that as long as the Werewolves were able to form pacts with enemy spirits, they were at a constant disadvantage. The Bale Hounds were useful allies, but in truth, the wolves were already on the look out for traitors in their midst. The spirits needed allies the Uratha would never see coming, and so they began to create their own shifters! At first, the plan worked like a charm, the Uratha were completely unprepared for ordinary humans that could spontaneously become shapeshifters. But eventually they discovered what was going on and were deeply troubled by the realization that spirits had such power.<br /> <br /> Fortunately (for the Uratha) only high ranking spirits of Vice seemed to manifest this ability. Weaker spirits were not able to infuse the essence of the beast with that of humans, but it was still considered to be a serious threat. Today, when a Beast of Vice is found operating in the territory, it's considered a warning signal that they have a powerful spirit who has gone rogue. If multiple species begin appearing, it means the Uratha are severely lacking in their ability to fulfill their duty!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Lust:</b> Werebunnies - Based on a type of animal who is infamous for its reproductive speed, the Werebunnies are sexual deviants. Their rampant promiscuity in the physical world creates large numbers of weaker lust spirits for their Totem to draw power from. Much like ordinary bunnies, they often become prey to the wolves once they're spotted. Many wolves mistake the bunnies for being easy prey, however, and forget just how fast they can move when scared, and how hard they can kick when they fight back! Werebunnies are often in service to spirits of perversion, euphoria, and even rape.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Pride:</b> Werebadgers - With the Honey Badger ranked as the most fearless animal on earth, Werebadgers are extremely arrogant and willing to take on all challengers. Fearless daredevils, the Werebadgers are protected from attacks on their mind and spirit by their own pride. Often at times, they are so arrogant, that those around them feel driven to knock them down a peg, attempting feats of greater bravery, and producing spirits of pride for their Totem to harvest from. Werebadgers are often in service to spirits of vanity, arrogance, and bravado<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Sloth:</b> Wereslugs - Some might call them mellow, others lethargic, but to put it more bluntly, they are lazy and unmotivated. Wereslugs tend to sit around and avoid any strenuous activity unless it becomes necessary to survival. This wouldn't present such a problem to the Werewolves, if their totems hadn't granted them the power to instil this laziness in others as well! When a Wereslug uses their powers, nobody seems motivated to do anything, and fall into a state of depression and hopelessness. Most Wereslugs can effect humans in such a manner, but those of considerable power are even capable of inflicting this state into the Uratha themselves, compelling them to ignore their oaths and their duty to Mother Luna! Wereslugs are often in service to spirits of lethargy, weakness, and doubt.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Greed:</b> Weresquirrels - Hoarding essences like they were acorns, the Weresquirrels gravitate towards Loci and begin scavenging whatever power they can from the place. When they are finally hunted down by the People, their Totems often have a nice stockpile of power to throw against them. Weresquirrels are often in service to spirits of thievery, avarice, and selfishness<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Envy:</b> Werevulture - Like the scavengers they resemble, the Werevulture seeks a moment of weakness so that they can swoop in and claim the prize for themselves. This prize can be anything the Werevulture might covet, whether it be a previously claimed lover, a promotion at work, a wealthy inheritance from a family member they never speak to, and so on. Although initially their Totems feed off spirits produced by the Werevulture itself, they eventually grow to a point in society where others become envious of them, thus providing a feast for their Totem! Werevultures are often in service to spirits of desire, betrayal, and suffering.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Wrath:</b> Werewolverine - Ordinary Wolverines are so violent, they have chased off much larger predators including bears and even wolves. Thinking that a Werewolverine can accomplish the same feat, they are often dispatched by their totems to engage the Werewolf population when they start becoming a problem. Werewolverines are often in service to spirits of violence, hatred, and destruction.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Gluttony:</b> Werelocusts - Ordinary locusts are capable of devouring great expanses of land when working as a swarm. A Werelocust is worse, possessed by a voracious appetite, they burn through essence at an accelerated rate (all related abilities cost x3) and so they are constantly hungry for more! This hunger keeps them firmly in the spirit's control rather than out of it. They wave essence in front of them in the same way one would a treat for their pets. After they perform whatever trick their masters demand of them, they are rewarded, but quickly they are hungry for more, begging for a new task, and (more importantly) another treat! Werelocusts are often in service to spirits of famine, addiction, and industry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-25-2014, 06:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-17T02:06:07-07:00","id":140349,"title":"","content":"Aww, I was hoping to read more creations than just what we have up now. ):","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-17T23:57:54-07:00","id":141291,"title":"","content":"My favorite was a remake of the snake-shifter, for a Wild West game. A rattle-snake (so very original, I know) sheriff. I don't recall the specifics, because this was awhile ago, but I know it had a nasty bite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2861-nyx","name":"Nyx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2861_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-18T01:03:05-07:00","id":141355,"title":"","content":"Best lawman ever. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-18T15:41:23-07:00","id":141887,"title":"","content":"<b>Weremoose + Werebeavers:</b> Two breeds of shapeshifters found in the regions of Canada. Having formed an alliance with each other, the Moose tend to spirits in the forest, while the Beavers tend to spirits near the water. While their relations with the Forsaken are mixed, they clearly dislike the Pure. Sometimes this mutual hatred leads to them forming allegiances with Forsaken packs if the Pure are becoming especially problematic.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Werefrogs:</b> Amphibious shapeshifters who display a society that is similar to the Gauru themselves. They even have their own auspices, which involve various species of frogs! Their society has three different auspices in total. The Bight Skins are colorful tree frogs with vibrant personalities, who typically serve as the diplomats and leaders of their people. Their suction tipped digits make it easier to climb when in their beast forms. The Deadly Skins are poisonous frogs with grim personalities, who typically serve as the shamans and assassins of their people. By spending essence while in their beast forms, they can secrete a deadly poison from their pours! Finally there are the Thick Skins who are various species of bull frogs and have gruff and serious personalities. They typically serve as the warriors of their people, inflating themselves to great sizes before descending upon the enemy!<br /> <br /> The Werefrogs are typically found in swamps and rainforests, where there is plenty of water to hide in.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Werechickens:</b> A breed of shapeshifters who are laughing stocks in the eyes of the Gauru. They are very rarely seen in the city, mostly being encountered out in the countryside. Their purpose is largely unknown, and given how pissed they are at the wolves, its possible they won't be finding out anytime soon. They do seem to have a particular attraction to spirits that deal with farming and agriculture, however.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Werepiranha:</b> Nightmarish shapeshifters who are native to the amazon and are extremely territorial. They didn't seem to get the memo that shapeshifters aren't supposed to eat people, and savagely devour anyone who comes within their watery domains. Their xenophobic rage has lead others to believe that they breed exclusively with their own kind, instead of taking humans as their mates. It is obvious they have taken evil spirits as their totems, but their barbaric displays make it near impossible to investigate further!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 05-18-2014, 06:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-19T18:04:27-07:00","id":142851,"title":"","content":"I have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/15828-new-shifter-kushtaka-the-trickster-wereotters\">Wereotters who weave loci</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-19T18:48:10-07:00","id":142931,"title":"","content":"New shifters are all well and good, but let's not forget Changing Breeds' lesson of the Were-Sugar Glider (one of the few positive things to come out of that book. Not that an occasional one-off is fine, but an entire breed of WereMoose? What would be the point? <br /> <br /> That being said, the <b>Vespa</b> wasp-women exist in a grey space between Shifter and Host, breeding among humanity, but in a cruel mirror of nature, also capable of breeding by infecting Azlu with their own Essence; the resultant offspring (effectively, a pseudo- Wasp Host) act as powerful guardians/priests of the Hive, the mythic progenitor of all breeds of Swarming Ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-19T19:18:55-07:00","id":142957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=142931#post142931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">New shifters are all well and good, but let's not forget Changing Breeds' lesson of the Were-Sugar Glider (one of the few positive things to come out of that book. Not that an occasional one-off is fine, but an entire breed of WereMoose? What would be the point?<br /> <br /> That being said, the <b>Vespa</b> wasp-women exist in a grey space between Shifter and Host, breeding among humanity, but in a cruel mirror of nature, also capable of breeding by infecting Azlu with their own Essence; the resultant offspring (effectively, a pseudo- Wasp Host) act as powerful guardians/priests of the Hive, the mythic progenitor of all breeds of Swarming Ones.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> What is a Sugar Glider? @_@<br /> <br /> <br /> And I now have a sudden urge to make a Werenarwhal.<br /> <br /> <br /> I don't understand what the problem is with a breed of Wheremoose. They've teamed up with the Werebeavers to patrol both the forest and the rivers and occasionally they clash or ally themselves with the wolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T09:48:28-07:00","id":145770,"title":"","content":"For my own game, my players and I have created Selkies (Were-seals), Children of Anansi (were-spiders) and Water-Horses (were-horse/fish/monster-y things, think kelpies and the like). You can find all three through my signature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>GM of the <i>Walking Shadow</i> Campaigns</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/8992-the-hacks-and-houserules-of-walking-shadows\" target=\"_blank\">New System and Setting Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1040-neotiamat","name":"NeoTiamat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1040_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"471"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T12:34:29-07:00","id":145923,"title":"","content":"I was actually thinking of some way to do a incorporate a Selkie, but wasn't sure if it would fit more with Werewolf or Changelings. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T22:43:13-07:00","id":146408,"title":"","content":"I had a sudden onset of IDEA a few days ago that won't go away.<br /> <br /> Were-horses.<br /> <br /> A first-generation werehorse is born to an ordinary horse and lives as such until maturity, when they undergo their first change and a period of rapid intellectual development. Fate seems to conspire that this process goes without interference. In fact, any visions or spirit-visitations that occur will go towards their human owners, to encourage them to help the werehorse adjust to his new existence. As a species they have no claim to any one mystical progenitor, and often adopt whatever religious beliefs of whatever humans raised them. In fact, they seem almost drawn to religion, and atheistic were-horses are almost unheard-of.<br /> <br /> Because of this, were-horses identify themselves according to their own religious and cultural beliefs, and a Herd may include an hindu <i>Ashvin </i>alongside a chinese <i>Kirin </i>and scottish <i>kelpie</i>. Philosophical differences usually prevent such diverse Herds from forming, however, though werehorses are quick to set aside differences and band together when something truly evil rears it head.<br /> <br /> Werehorses are acutely aware of unnatural disturbances in the shadow, and focus their efforts on repairing damage to the function of the spirit-world, such as fighting the Hosts and repairing wounds. They do not uphold a specific agenda in regards to spirits, and individual werehorses will help clear woodland to build new homes just as quickly as they would sabotage an illegal logging operation. <br /> <br /> (insert blurb on common spirit gifts)<br /> <br /> A final oddity of werehorses is this - while their transformed shapes do inflict a form of lunacy upon onlookers, the effect is dependent upon the werehorse's and the viewer's humanity score. High-humanity (8+) humans are filled with instinctive respect and trust when viewing a transformed werehorse with an equally high harmony score, though they will grow suspicious, fearful, or even enraged at the same sight as the werehorse's harmony score diminishes. Likewise, a low-humanity (3-) viewer seeing a high-harmony werehorse is filled with instinctive terror and guilt, as if the creature they see 'knows' that they have done something wrong, and seeks to punish them.<br /> <br /> So, basically horse-angel things with a patron that works in mysterious ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1457-quanutmshard","name":"Quanutmshard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1457_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T06:23:16-07:00","id":146624,"title":"","content":"Alright, I wrote up a rough draft for the Werebunnies. I based the sheet off Neo Tiamat's Water-Horse design. Now granted that I don't have Changing Breeds or War Against the Pure, I don't know about the various Renowns and Gifts that non Uratha might have access to, so I left that blank. I'm also unsure whether or not I made the form buffs balanced or not, so I'd appreciate some feedback or advice on how to make them better if possible. I'm also working on Werevultures as the second of the list.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>NOTE:</b> I had originally intended to write all 7 Beasts of Vice in one sheet. However, I realized the idea I had for them was too diverse to do so, and so I've split each of the 7 Beasts of Vice into their own sections.<br /> <br /> ---------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Beasts of Lust (Werebunnies):</b> Before the First Change, Werebunnies are people who displayed an abundance of sexual promiscuity. Whores, players, porn stars, strippers, nymphomaniacs, perverts, all of these and more fit the back story of many Beasts of Lust. Eventually their lustful ways attracted the attention of a Spirit of Lust who initiates the First Change either willingly or forcing it upon them depending upon the mortal's reaction to the situation.<br /> <br /> The First Change for a Werebunny is... mind blowing, to say the least. Their body is wracked with ecstasy as they writhe on the ground, tearing at their clothes. Their mind swims with sexual fantasies, their words escape as moans of euphoria, and their vision blurs with tears of joy. By the time the First Change wears off, the Werebunny is usually left panting naked on the floor, drenched in sweat and their lower regions covered in sexual fluids from their own spontaneous orgasms. When they finally wake up, they remember very little about what happened during that time. All they know is that it was fucking awesome, and they want more!<br /> <br /> <br /> Werebunnies are notorious for their sexual urges and are constantly looking for ways to indulge themselves. Every time they do so, it produces a newborn spirit of lust, which their Totem can either devour, absorb, or bind into service in order to increase its own power. Every lover they seduce, every orgasm they achieve, every bastard they conceive strengthens their Totem spirit. As such, those who agreed to the change are not ashamed of their actions, for they know that their powers will grow from the experience. For those who had the change forced upon them, however, they begin to feel like sex slaves. Unable to keep their urges under control for more than a few hours at a time, their Totem's voice constantly demanding them to go out and fulfill their duty, and often they don't have the strength of will to resist such compulsions.<br /> <br /> <br /> Unlike the Uratha who disguise their tribes as reclusive societies or cultural heritage living on sacred land, the Beasts of Lust make their Locus in full view of the public. They take up residence in night clubs, brothels, whore houses, anywhere that attracts mortals looking for a good time. Their Totem spirits are often housed somewhere inside these Loci, usually underground where they feed on the sexual energy of the patrons who visit while the Werebunnies pose as workers.<br /> <br /> Hiding in plain sight gives the Werebunnies more security than you might think. Their Loci are often hotspots for public entertainment, and unlike the Pure living on the borders of human civilization, a pack of Forsaken can not march in and begin slaughtering the place without causing all sorts of trouble and backlash in the process. The Werebunnies are not warriors, they are lovers, and while they may possess some degree of fighting skill, it usually pales in comparison to the Uratha who kill supernatural threats on a regular basis. They use their patrons like human shields, knowing the Uratha can not openly challenge them with so many people around to act as witnesses. Lunacy may have its perks, but enough people come up with a similar story to one another, and something is bound to stick.<br /> <br /> <br /> As with all Beasts of Vice, the Werebunnies have extended lifespans beyond the normal human capacity. Although they are not immortal, they age at 1/3 the normal rate, physically aging by 1 year for every 3 that goes by. This allows them to retain their youth and beauty for longer periods of time, and thus create more lustful spirits for their Totem to draw power from. An elder Werebunny is rare, but can live to see the early 300's before age finally claims them.<br /> <br /> Simply put, the Werebunnies are one of the more passive aggressive enemies the Uratha must face. They would prefer not to fight at all if they had a choice in the matter, but the fact that they are serving dark spirits and threatening the balance of the world means they have to be dealt with, as Luna's will dictates.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b> Unlike many other shapeshifters, the Werebunnies do not appear frightening in any of their forms. They're certainly shocking to come across in certain forms, but their bodies seem to be more alluring than frightening.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Human:</b> In their human form, the Werebunny appears as a very attractive and enticing human. Even if they were ugly in their original human forms, a Werebunny awakens from the First Change to find they have become incredibly sexy. Their skin is unblemished by acne, warts, skin tags, bruises, or scars, they have lost their excess body weight as they slim down to a more attractive figure. Male Werebunnies are commonly known to take on human forms that are nearly androgynous in their physical appearance, consisting of reduced muscle mass, wider hips, and pout lips. It is a common belief that Werebunny males are gay, or at the very least bisexual due to their appearance in this form.<br /> <br /> Even when they start to show signs of physical age, a Werebunny's human form manages to retain its beauty. They only barely show signs of wrinkles, and while their hair might pale a few shades, it never turns gray or white. Their body retain its curvy sex appeal, and they typically resemble an extremely healthy individual for a man / woman of their age. Such vigorous appearances are practically essential to their survival, as a feeble and decrepit body would make it impossible to satisfy their Primal Urge.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Animal:</b> In their animal form, the Werebunny looks like typical bunny of various species that differs according to the individual. As with most Beasts of Vice, those who were forced to become a Beast of Lust against their will feel more comfortable in their animal form and resort to it as their default shape when not interacting with others.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Hybrid:</b> In their hybrid form, the Werebunny takes on the shape of a voluptuous and very attractive anthropomorphic bunny. Those who are not stricken with Lunacy might wonder as to how this form could ever be considered threatening or referred to as a &quot;war form.&quot; Their hybrid form is roughly the same size as their human form, unlike other shifters who grow into monstrous sizes. The Werebunny's sexual assets receive an increase in the form of an extra cup size for females, and 3 inches added to their manhood for males. Since their body size remains mostly the same, they can usually hide these features with clothing, but by the time they assume this form, they usually aren't too concerned about modesty anymore. Their leg muscles do become firmer and stronger, however, allowing them to sprint great distances, leap with the ease of Olympic athletes, and deliver one hell of a nasty kick to anyone who tries to give them trouble.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Near Animal:</b> In their near animal form, the Werebunny takes the form of a freakishly large bunny who is 50% larger than their human form. The bunny's eyes display a keen intelligence on par with that of a human, and it seems to have an understanding of human speech and technology when presented with such, but otherwise looks like a giant bunny.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Near Human:</b> In their near human form, the Werebunny takes a form that resembles something out of a perverted anime series. They gain the ears, tail, and dexterous paws of their hybrid form, but the rest of their body retains its human appearance. Their leg muscles strengthen, but not the same degree as their hybrid state. Many of these features can be hidden with special clothing, so the near human form is good for increasing one's fighting abilities without going full hybrid in public.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> As with all Beast of Vice, the Werebunnies were born as ordinary humans but were infused with the essence of an animal by a powerful dark spirit. In this case, they were infused with the essence of a bunny by a Spirit of Lust. Werebunnies are also the most sexually active of all the Beasts of Vice and are the most prone to conceiving children who 99% of the time are bastards.<br /> <br /> When a Werebunny produces a child with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance they will inherit a profound manifestation of their parent's Vice. If they produce a child with another Werebunny, this risk increases to a 60% chance for each child produced. Regardless of who they mate with, the child is born as a human, but may have a higher affinity to the spirit world than most. Children who manifest Lust in such a way are more vulnerable to the Totem's influence and are often targeted sometime around puberty to be granted the First Change.<br /> <br /> Werebunnies who freely accepted the change will often raise their children into the tribe like a sort of family religion. Indoctrinating them from an early age into the Spirit Totem's will. Those who had the change forced upon them might attempt to warn their child or abandon them at an orphanage in a desperate attempt to spare them the same fate.<br /> <br /> Marriage for the Beasts of Lust is an alien concept to them. They serve a spirit of lust, not love, and so most of their sexual encounters are done for the purpose of entertainment as opposed to romantic attachments. It is possible that if they are particularly attracted to someone, they may choose to mate with them repeatedly, but once they start to lose interest, they usually moved on to their next lover.<br /> <br /> Incest is also popular among Werebunnies who freely accepted the First Change. The more experienced family members help the new ones adjust to the powerful urges they're feeling until they learn how to properly control themselves. Then they are deemed ready for human interaction, considering how these families often indoctrinate their children from young ages, this also has the effect of making pedophilia a common trait among such families. Which sickens those who rejected the First Change, and claim that those who accepted it have no sense of decency.<br /> <br /> <br /> Because they are born as humans, a Werebunny pack can never be truly destroyed so long as the Totem remains. Even if every living member of the pack is killed, the Totem can simply create some new replacements. The only way to truly stop the Werebunnies from creating more of themselves is to destroy the Totem itself!<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Euphoria - Those who witness a Werebunny in its hybrid, near human or near animal forms or witness it shapeshifting are not afflicted with madness like typical Lunacy. However, their minds are overcome with a overwhelming sense of euphoric attraction and pleasure as they are drawn to the Werebunny. When the effect wears off, it is nearly impossible to recall events beyond hazy, dream-like flash backs, which makes explaining things to the authorities too obscure to be credible evidence.<br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Survival Rage - Werebunnies are not warriors by nature, but as with any member of the animal kingdom, they will fight back in desperation if they are pushed far enough. When a Werebunny is forced into a Rage, they are not lashing out in a desire to tear people to shreds, but rather a desire to escape the situation with their lives. They will most likely try to flee the moment they clear a path to do so, rather than stay behind and finish the job.<br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> As the servants of lustful spirits, Werebunnies have extraordinarily high sexual desires. They must slake their lust a number of times a day equal to 1/2 of their Primal Urge with a minimum of once a day. If they fail to satisfy this quota, they will continue to lose 1 Willpower each day which can not be regained until they have satisfied themselves. If their Willpower reaches 0 from such a method, they will immediately fly into a Sexual Rage that lasts until double their appropriate quota has been achieved.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> Human - None<br /> <br /> Near Human - +1 strength (leg related actions), +1 Dexterity, +1 Athletics (Running + Jumping), +2 Perception (sound)<br /> <br /> Hybrid Form - +2 strength (leg related actions), +2 Dexterity, +2 Athletics (Running + Jumping), +3 Speed, +4 Perception (sound)<br /> <br /> Animal Form - +1 strength (leg related actions), +1 Dexterity, +4 Athletics (Running), +2 Athletics (Jumping), +5 Speed, +5 Perception (Sound), Size -3<br /> <br /> Near Animal Form - +2 strength (leg related actions), +2 Dexterity, +3 Athletics (Running), +2 Athletics (Jumping), +4 Perception (Sound)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-26-2014, 06:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T07:10:53-07:00","id":146689,"title":"","content":"LOL @ moar strength than Uratha &amp; Sexual Raeg.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T07:54:36-07:00","id":146734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=146689#post146689\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">LOL @ moar strength than Uratha &amp; Sexual Raeg.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I suppose I should go back and read my core book to see how strong they are in comparison. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: Alright, I think I balanced it out now. I tried to make them weaker than Uratha, since they're not accustomed to fighting. Their stats are supposed to be geared towards escaping first, and fighting as a last resort.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 05-23-2014, 11:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T12:31:14-07:00","id":148046,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of the Werebunnies. I'd also love to see the rest of the Vice Shifters if you had ideas about their stats.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","name":"Darkhunter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar213_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T13:17:27-07:00","id":148083,"title":"","content":"I'm working on a breed of Were-Owl for a crossover game that have an instinctive hatred of Kindred. Why? Because the first of their kind was the result of a Strix-Possessed Mortal birthing a child. The Owls themselves do not know of their ties to The Strix, however faint it may be now, but the hatred of Kindred burns strong - some say it is growing stronger with each generation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/128x125q90/90/w20supportergarou125.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1612-mabs","name":"Mabs","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1612_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"78"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T14:42:48-07:00","id":148144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkhunter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=148046#post148046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the idea of the Werebunnies. I'd also love to see the rest of the Vice Shifters if you had ideas about their stats.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do, I'm working on Werevultures next, and after that will probably be Werelocusts. I've also decided to replace Weresloths with Wereslugs, as making a Sloth to represent Sloth seemed a tad predictable to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T15:06:53-07:00","id":148171,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Werebunny's sexual assets receive an increase in the form of an extra cup size for females, and 3 inches added to their manhood for males. </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, but while exaggerated sexual characteristics are certainly in-theme with what you're writing...I'm just slightly discombobulated that you felt the need to be so...<i>specific... </i>about the effects.<br /> <br /> Although it's certainly less disturbing than if you'd felt the need to use a formula. <br /> <br /> (boobs/schlong = Primal Urge + stamina + size, anyone?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1457-quanutmshard","name":"Quanutmshard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1457_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T16:00:27-07:00","id":148203,"title":"","content":"I don't know how that would translate into the game's formulae (it sounds like it could by anywhere from 5 to 20 inches increased), and I didn't flat out say boobs and shlong. I'm new at this, so I'm going by what I think is appropriate. Besides the others won't be quite as sexually based. In fact, the Vultures and Slugs are going to be quite the opposite. All I need to do is figure out the right stat enhancements to give their forms and the vultures should be ready to post.<br /> <br /> <br /> What I'm trying to capture for these shifter species is that they are not something you would actually want to become. Other shifters have a proud, cultural heritage, but these are people who became targeted by evil spirits because of their inability to control themselves. So it's intended to have more drawbacks and humiliation than normal. For Werebunnies, this means becoming sexual deviants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 05-24-2014, 08:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T16:21:15-07:00","id":148209,"title":"","content":"<b>Beasts of Envy (Werevultures):</b> Before the First Change, Werevultures are people who are stricken with jealousy and desire things that those around them already possess. They often come from the ranks of the poverty stricken, the oppressed and the powerless, scorned lovers, and rival siblings. Their jealousy attracts the attention of a Spirit of Envy who initiates the First Change either willingly or forcing it upon them depending upon the mortal's reaction to the situation.<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change for a Werevulture throws them into an uncontrollable fit of jealousy. They look at what others have and long to have it for themselves. During the First Change, it is possible they will become so envious, they are willing to kill people in a jealous rage and take the object of their desire. Whether that be food, possessions, territory, or even a lover! When the First Change wears off, the Werevulture awakens to find themselves surrounded by objects they have always valued but were never able to acquire on their own. Most of these objects are stolen, of course, and the new Werevulture may end up having to hide out until things blow over.<br /> <br /> <br /> Werevultures are jealous creatures who constantly want what others possess. Like true scavengers, however, they wait for an opportunity to take it. If there is somebody that they lust for, they will secretly wait for a break up to occur (or cause one) and swoop in upon the heart broken lover during a time of emotional vulnerability. If they are hungry for food, they have little restraint in eating the table scraps of those who are better fed than themselves, although Werevultures who had the change forced upon them may be disgusted with their actions. If they have a wealthy family member, they might orchestrate their demise, and then show up with the rest of the family when it's time to collect their inheritance. Werevultures very rarely steal from others publicly. Instead they hide in the shadows and bide their time, waiting for an opportunity or they orchestrate one themselves. They are like the vultures circling high in the sky, waiting for the prey to show a moment of weakness, then they swoop down to scavenge the remains.<br /> <br /> Initially serving their Totem spirit is time consuming and difficult. Most Werevultures had nothing when they were human, and so the spirit has to devour, fuse with, or bind envious spirits that the Werevulture personally creates. Over time, however, the Werevulture continues to improve their lot in life until they reach a point where other people become jealous of them instead. This begins producing a larger abundance of envious spirits for their Totem to draw power from. It can take years before a Werevulture is able to stake their claim on a particular Locus, as they do not have the power or the resources to maintain control of it on their own. However, as their powers increase and more Werevultues join their pack, they find that controlling Loci becomes easier. These Loci are usually poverty stricken districts where people are in desperate need and yet often ignored by those who have. However, it is also possible for them to create a Loci in places of abundant wealth and prosperity, mostly if these areas are populated by two faced liars who are constantly fighting with each other over land and possessions.<br /> <br /> Werevultures feel an instinctive desire to observe their surroundings from a high altitude. If a Werevulture possessed acrophobia when they were human, they awake from the First Change to find themselves cured of such a weakness. Werevultures commonly patrol the city from the roof tops, gazing down upon the streets and alleyways below and tracking their prey without being detected. Like real birds, they are also incredibly difficult to catch by simply chasing after them. The Werevultures can spot the Werewolves down on the ground and usually escape before they can reach the top of the building. The best chance of capturing them is to wait for one of them to come down, often with the help of setting out some bait to attract their attention. Hopefully the Werevulture is too focused on scavenging to notice the Wolves lurking in the shadows, ready to pounce on the grounded bird.<br /> <br /> Werevultures may seem like they aren't much of a threat, being simple scavengers who pick up the scraps left over by society. However their true threat lies in the potential danger of what they can do to society. They need humans to be envious and jealous in order to appease their Totem, and when people are envious and jealous for long periods of time, they often become... unstable. Civility is often discarded in the face of desperation, and if left unchecked, a pack of Werevultures can inadvertently cause rioting in the city streets as the desperate citizens begin demanding for a better change to their lives. They have been oppressed and ignored for too long and now they demand to be heard! It is the possibility of unleashing this anarchy and throwing the district into extreme disharmony that the Werewolves fear, and it is for this reason that they hunt the Beasts of Envy.<br /> <br /> Ironically enough, the envious Werevultures possess one special privilege that few others get to share, they have the unbridled freedom that comes with flight! Shapeshifters who are confined to the ground look up with envy at the Werevultures who are free to roam the skies, disconnected from the troubles of the world below. It is a gift that they flaunt, for their Totem is able to feed upon the envy it creates.<br /> <br /> As with all Beasts of Vice, Werevultures have an extended lifespan beyond normal human capacity. They age at 1/5th the normal rate, physically aging by 1 year for every 5 that goes by. Their extended lifespans means they are left to suffer in their jealous envy for longer periods of time, providing even greater levels of essence and spirits for their Totem to draw upon. An elder Werevulture can potentially live up to 500 years, but virtually all known cases of elder Werevultures display some kind of mental derangement brought upon by extensive periods of desperation, jealousy, and hopelessness.<br /> <br /> <br /> -------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> In their human form, Werevultures often resemble pathetic wretches who have a reason to be jealous of other people. In contrast to the Werebunnies who become more beautiful after the First Change, the Werevultures find they have become uglier. Their skin shows signs of premature aging such as wrinkles, baldness, spots, and lack of muscle mass. It is easier for Werevultures to blend in with society's elderly than it is for them to hang around the young and the beautiful.<br /> <br /> <br /> In their animal form, the Werevulture takes the shape of a larger than normal bird of prey, specifically the vulture. This form is roughly half the size of their human form and grants them the ability to fly! As befits this form, the Werevulture gains incredible perception in the form of sight and hearing.<br /> <br /> <br /> In their Hybrid Form, the Werevulture becomes a huge, anthropomorphic vulture standing around 8 feet tall. They have a set of wings growing from their back which although they can not actually fly with, they do have the power to glide. Their hands become like a secondary pair of talons, and their face protrudes into a vicious beak that can easily strip flesh from bone. Their neck elongates, providing them greater flexibility and the power to make lunging bite attacks instead of having to grapple their prey.<br /> <br /> <br /> In their near human form, the Werevulture becomes even uglier than their human form. Their nose extends in length and becomes hook shaped as their skin becomes wrinkled and clings to their withered body. They stand in a hunched over posture and their fingernails become a set of claws, growing two inches in length and developing a curved shape like the talons of a bird. Their voice takes on a hoarse and raspy tone to it, not unlike those who are trying to imitate the squawking of a bird.<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction</b>: Werevultures rarely have the chance to actually breed with others because they are usually not the object of someone's affection unless they exploit their emotions in some way. On the rare chance that a Werevulture does get the opportunity to produce a biological child, it will be born as a human, although it may exhibit a higher affinity to the spirit world than normal.<br /> <br /> If a Werevulture mates with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance the child will exhibit a profound manifestation of their parent's Vice. This chance increases to 60% for each child if they mate with a fellow Werevulture. As with all Beasts of Vice, these types of progeny are the favored targets of their Totem spirit and will more than likely be selected to join the pack, willingly or not.<br /> <br /> It is far more common, however, that Werevultures are single generations. Meaning they have no prior or subsequent connection to Werevultures, because the rarity in which they find willing mates. When a Werevulture attempts to find a mate, they almost never go after someone who is available. Instead, they look for someone who is already taken, because their jealousy compels them to seek out the fortune of others and find some way to take it from them. An open, willing, and eager mate runs the risk of making a Werevulture content, and that is something which their Totem simply can not allow. So they guide the Werevulture's affections towards something better suited to their tastes, often without the Werevulture even knowing about it.<br /> <br /> Like all Beasts of Vice, the threat of a Werevulture pack can not be truly eliminated so long as their Totem remains active. Even if every member of the pack is killed, the Totem can simply create some new replacements, so their enemies must destroy the Totem itself to stop their ability to reproduce.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Jealousy - Those who witness a Werevulture in their near human, near animal, or hybrid forms will suddenly begin to obsess over things which they covet but yet belong to other people. They are so focused on their desires to own these belongings that they ignore what is going on around them and instead may be focused on creating plans, bargains, trades, or heists for how to obtain the objects of their desire. When Jealousy wears off, the subject will come to their senses to realize they have very little memory of what occurred during that time, since they were not paying attention to their surroundings.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns</b>:<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Jealous Rage - Sometimes plans don't work out the way you want them to, or they take too long to get the job done. When a Werevulture feels that they have been denied, oppressed, or suffered too much, they are prone to flying into a murderous envy. During this state, they will attempt to take what they want by force, even if that means they have to kill the current owner to get it. When multiple Werevultures are raging at the same time, it can look like a riot is occurring as they smash windows, loot stores, and beat the crap out of rival lovers.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> Werevultures are scavengers and often the dregs of society. They feed on the scraps left behind by others. As part of their Primal Urge, the Werevultures are constantly seeking to improve their lot in life and rise through the ranks of society. When a Werevulture sees an opportunity to obtain something that they want and does not act upon it, they suffer a -2 on all Physical, Mental, or Social rolls (Story Teller's Choice) for the next 24 hours. This weakness can stack with itself if the Werevulture continues to deny future opportunities, weakening several categories or focusing on a single category to make them guaranteed to fail!<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> Human - None<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Human - Strength +1, Dexterity +2, Stamina +1, Size +1, Apearance -2, Inflicts lethal damage with talon attacks.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hybrid Form - Size +1, Health +3, Dexterity +2, Strength +2, Stamina +2, Perception (Sight) +3, inflicts lethal damage with bite and talon attacks.<br /> <br /> Glide - The Werevulture in their Hybrid Form is able to glide up to a distance equal to Strength + Dexterity yards<br /> <br /> <br /> Animal Form - Size -2, Dexterity +1, Speed +2, Perception (Sight) +5, inflicts lethal damage with bite and talon strikes.<br /> <br /> Flight - A Werevulture in their animal form is able to fly at a rate of speed = yards per turn.<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Animal Form - Size +1, Dexterity +2, Strength +1, Stamina +1, Perception (Sight) +5, inflicts lethal damage with bite and talon attacks.<br /> <br /> Flight - A Werevulture in their near animal form can fly at a rate of speed = yards per turn","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 05-24-2014, 08:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-24T16:49:16-07:00","id":148254,"title":"","content":"This thread makes me very happy. Along side the Were-Spiders from the Ettumu project, Kushtaka the Were-Otters, fan Hosts from a thread on this forum and stuff cherry picked from Predators/War Against The Pure/Skin Changers, my Werewolf games might be able to be larger and stranger than what people might see from the books they own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":213,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","user":"Darkhunter","date":".  Spelling 05-24-2014, 07:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","name":"Darkhunter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar213_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-25T01:12:06-07:00","id":148520,"title":"","content":"<b>Beasts of Sloth (Wereslugs):</b> Before the first change, Wereslugs are normally people who wouldn't pose much of a threat. They are lazy, unmotivated, uncooperative and have a tendency to do things half-assed. Their lack of effort and enthusiasm attracts the attention of a spirit of Sloth, who initiates the First Change either willingly or forcing it upon them. Usually by force in this particular case!<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change for a Wereslug is rather unimpressive. Their mind and body are overcome with an overwhelming sense of lethargy. Their limbs feel too heavy to move, and they can barely keep their eyes open. A Beast of Sloth can awaken from the first change to find they've barely moved from the same spot in the last several hours. And there is also one other trait the Wereslugs awaken to find which more often than not ends up causing them to faint again. After undergoing the First Change, the Wereslug becomes a hermaphrodite, developing the genitals of the opposite gender in addition to the ones they already possessed.<br /> <br /> Wereslugs who were male prior to the First Change have a male dominant personality and refer to themselves by the male prefix. Conversely, Wereslugs who were female prior to the First Change have a female dominant personality and refer to themselves by the female prefix. Male Wereslugs do not develop breasts, so it is easy to tell who is &quot;male&quot; and who is &quot;female&quot; among them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The major threat that a Wereslug poses to the Uratha is not their strategic planning or their combat effectiveness. It is their ability to instil people with exhaustion, doubt, hopelessness. A Wereslug is able to win battles without ever having to lift a finger, draining their opponents of their will to fight back and making them easy prey. A powerful Wereslug can even do this to the Uratha themselves, causing them to neglect their Oaths and fail to uphold their duty to the spirit world! Such laziness creates more spirits of sloth who their Totem can devour, fuse with, or bind into servitude in order to increase its own power!<br /> <br /> <br /> Out of all the Beasts of Vice, the Wereslugs have the longest extended lifespans and far exceed humans in their longevity. They are not immortal, but they do age at 1/10th the normal rate. For every 10 years that goes by, a Wereslug ages by only 1. Near the end of their lifespan, a Wereslug can approach 1,000 years of age. More often than not, they need this much time in order to accomplish anything worth while for their Totem masters. Wereslugs do not feel particularly enthusiastic to go out and start riling up a pack of wolves. They are more content to indulge opportunities as they come across them, rather than actively seeking them out. As such, it can take years, possibly even decades for a Wereslug to cause any major damage to the area.<br /> <br /> <br /> Unfortunately, this also makes Wereslugs particularly difficult to track. Most Uratha expect an enemy to be immediately noticeable, either charging in guns blazing or hatching some kind of intricate plan to take over the city. Wereslugs are very mellow and care free, so they pass under the Uratha's radar. However, this is also a manifestation of their Totem's influence. The Uratha do not consider them a major threat (yet), and so they choose to ignore them. A fine example of Sloth, if ever there was one!<br /> <br /> <br /> At first glance, the Wereslugs may appear to be passive aggressive like the Werebunnies, but this is not the case. Werebunnies are all about having fun, but the job of a Wereslug is to instil people with apathy. Novelists begin to suffer prolonged bouts of writer's block, athletes start to deviate from their training regiments or give up on their diets, police officers become more open to bribery or ignoring protocol in favor of a quicker (though sloppier) method of getting the job done. Construction crews may overlook important safety designs, resulting in unstable and hazardous work environments. Wereslugs have the power to destroy people's lives without having to actually kill them, which makes them more dangerous and hostile than their long eared counterparts.<br /> <br /> <br /> Because a single Wereslug takes so long to get anything done, it is common for them to exist in large packs. Their Totem feels that if they have more Wereslugs working for them, it will increase their performance rate. The average size of a Wereslug pack measures between 10 to 20 members. Common locations for their Loci include homeless shelters, soup kitchens, back alleyways, and other places where you'll find those who are unemployed beggars who either can't (or won't) get a job. In fact, most of the inhabitants of these places are probably Wereslugs or potential candidates to join the pack.<br /> <br /> <br /> It should also be noted that Salt is a particularly deadly Bane to Wereslugs. If it makes contact with their skin, it immediately causes 1 Aggravated damage per inch of exposure. If the salt is ingested, it inflicts 1 Aggravated damage per gram that was consumed. Wereslugs must constantly monitor their meals to ensure it has not been seasoned with salt lately.<br /> <br /> <br /> -------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Human:</b> It is a common stereotype that all Wereslugs are obese slobs with poor hygiene in their human forms. While this is certainly the case for many of them, it is not universal. Some Wereslugs are actually quite healthy and fit. Instead their sloth manifests in a lack of attention to detail and favoring the easy path rather than dedicating themselves to hard work and effort. They have the shoot first, ask questions later approach. Wereslugs who live up to the stereotype, however, are surprisingly in tuned with their animal side. They are overweight, slow, have queasy stares, and have poor hygiene as their body gives off foul odors or is permeated by a coating of sweat.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Animal:</b> In their fully animal form, the Wereslug breaks down into a swarm of slugs numbering roughly 10 per rank of spirit power they have achieved (maximum of 50 slugs at Rank 5). The slugs act with one mind, and their attributes are counted for the entire swarm. When all their health boxes are destroyed, the swarm is dead. As long as at least one of the slugs survives, the Wereslug can reform themselves, however.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Hybrid:</b> In their Slug-Man form, the Wereslug's body becomes soft and mushy as their eyes rise out of the sockets and are supported upon two moveable stalks. They grow a massive tail which is roughly twice as large as their body and drags across the ground, reducing their speed as they struggle to pull it. Their teeth recede into their body as their mouth becomes perfectly circular, forming a sphincter shaped hole in their face. Their skin also becomes covered in a thick coating of slime, which they leave a trail of when they move. In other words, they become extremely hideous!<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Near Animal:</b> If their Animal Form wasn't already enough to shock people, their Near Animal Form certainly will. The Wereslug takes the shape of an unnaturally large slug roughly 50% larger than their human form. At best, they might be mistaken for some kind of prehistoric missing link. But more than likely they'll inflict normal witnesses with some kind of Lunacy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Near Human:</b> If a Wereslug wasn't ugly in their human form, they certainly become so in their Near Human form. Their body begins to bloat in size, swelling to obese proportions. Their pupils dialate to freakish sizes, taking up nearly 80% their eye sockets. Their skin also begins to emit a thick, gooey sweat, and their muscles become soft and flabby, rather than the firm tone they might have as a human.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Because of their condition Wereslugs often manifest bisexual tendencies and have even been known to take members of their own gender to be their mates at times. Children who have a Wereslug for a parent have a higher chance of being born with gender deformities than if both parents were humans. Like all Beasts of Vice, their children are born human, but may display a higher affinity to the spirit world than normal.<br /> <br /> When a Wereslug mates with a human, there is a 1/3 chance the child will manifest a profound version of the parent's vice. This chance increases to 60% per child if they mate with a fellow Wereslug. Children who display this have a higher chance of being targeted by the Totem to become a Wereslug themselves.<br /> <br /> While a Wereslug may be physically capable of breeding, many of them have no interest in doing so. Even if they find someone who doesn't consider them too ugly or lazy and is willing to have a child with them, the Wereslug is often at times not ready for a long term commitment. Raising a child takes work, patience, and a lengthy attention span, something the Wereslugs simply do not possess, and so it ends up becoming more of a chore to them than a blessing when they conceive a child.<br /> <br /> Indeed, much like the Werevultures, the majority of Wereslugs are single generations, meaning they were the first in their family to undergo the First Change. All Wereslugs, however, are capable of bearing children, even those who refer to themselves as male, but the male variant has even less interest in doing so than the female variants<br /> <br /> <br /> ------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Disconnection - When someone witnesses a Wereslug in their near human, near animal, or hybrid forms, they are overcome with a sense of disconnection from the situation at hand. Yeah, there's a giant mutant slug in front of them, but they don't particularly care. After all, it's a big world and there are bound to be strange things in it, right? In fact, the witnesses begin to find they don't really care about anything at the moment. They're much more interested in laying down and relaxing, they've had a hard time this month and they haven't been getting enough rest. When the effects of Disconnection wear off, the subject usually finds themselves unable to bring up details about what they saw. Not because they can't remember it, but because they don't feel like it's vital information that needs to be shared. Like most Lunacy rolls, the subject has to make a check to see if they can summon up the willpower to discuss what they witnessed, and even then, they usually share only bits and pieces, rather than the full account.<br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Death Rage - Slothful doesn't always mean slow, it can also mean doing things fast and sloppy. When a Wereslug enters into a rage, they attempt to resolve their current problem in the fastest possible manner they have available to them. It's neither pretty nor perfect, but as long as it gets the fucking job done, they don't care.<br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> Wereslugs are usually easy going and laid back individuals who don't like to exert themselves if they can help it. They grow agitated quickly when they're forced to put forth strenuous effort. As such, if a Wereslug fails to perform the same action 3 times in a row within the same scene, they will automatically fly into a Death Rage as they attempt to force the situation to a speedy conclusion.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> Human: None<br /> <br /> Hybrid: Size+2, Speed -3, Strength +3, Dexterity +1, Health +3, Climbing +2<br /> <br /> Animal: Size -3, Speed -5, Health +1, Dexterity +2, Climbing +4<br /> <br /> Near Animal: Size+1, Speed -3, Health +2, Dexterity +1, Climbing +3<br /> <br /> Near Human: Size+1, Speed -1, Health +1, Strength +2, Dexterity +1, Climbing +1","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-28-2014, 04:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-25T18:57:57-07:00","id":149062,"title":"","content":"Just a thought.<br /> <br /> Who thinks the Kappa from Japanese mythology could be converted into a Weretortoise?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T00:37:31-07:00","id":149199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=149062#post149062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a thought.<br /> <br /> Who thinks the Kappa from Japanese mythology could be converted into a Weretortoise?</div> </div> </div> </div>That would be Rather good interpretation and could make for a hybrid form &quot;oops&quot; of someone that remembers a WereTortoise without being effected by Lunacy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","name":"Darkhunter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar213_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T08:55:35-07:00","id":149429,"title":"","content":"So I just went and did some research. Kappa apparently are known for stealing a person's <i>shirikodama. </i>A mythical ball said to contain a person's soul..<br /> <br /> It's located in the anus!<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T10:13:04-07:00","id":149473,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=149429#post149429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I just went and did some research. Kappa apparently are known for stealing a person's <i>shirikodama. </i>A mythical ball said to contain a person's soul..<br /> <br /> It's located in the anus!<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Anal rapers?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","name":"Darkhunter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar213_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T10:15:53-07:00","id":149475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkhunter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=149473#post149473\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Anal rapers?</div> </div> </div> </div><i>*Rappers.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></i><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T13:47:31-07:00","id":149706,"title":"","content":"Mythology is fun!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T19:29:02-07:00","id":149914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=149706#post149706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mythology is fun!</div> </div> </div> </div><img src=\"http://img2.wikia.nocookie.net/__cb20081023004830/uncyclopedia/images/7/7c/Themoreyouknow.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/213-darkhunter","name":"Darkhunter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar213_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-31T01:04:36-07:00","id":154613,"title":"","content":"<b>Weredragons</b> - An ancient and powerful breed of shifter who trace their lineage all the way back to the early days of Atlantis. There is still much debate over whether they are true shapeshifters, or some ancient Legacy of Thyrsus who became especially versed in the spirit realm.<br /> <br /> <br /> What do you guys think? Plausible?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T15:20:49-07:00","id":156010,"title":"","content":"(While I'm still fleshing out the others in this section, decided to post up the general information on the BoV shifters)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Beasts of Vice:</b> In many tribal cultures throughout the world, shapeshifters are honored and revered beings. However this is not always the case in many of the old ways. You've probably heard stories of shifters who did not alter their forms by personal choice. Stories like the Swan Princess who was cursed to the form of a swan and could only assume her human form during the full moon. Or the story of a beggar having been turned away at the door and the next day the home owners are changed into hideous monsters.<br /> <br /> These stories are many, but in this particular case, we will be looking over the Beasts of Vice, mortals who's selfish urges cursed them with a form more appropriate to their behavior. In other cases, however, they can be mortals who willingly entered into a pact, sacrificing their human forms in exchange for power. To say that the Beasts of Vice are a unified culture is untrue. While they do share many similarities with one another, they are not exactly allies with one another. They ally themselves with evil spirits, and more often than not, these spirits are not looking to share their power with a potential rival, so the Beasts of Vice clash with each other just as much as they do with the Forsaken or the Pure.<br /> <br /> There are 7 different types of Beasts of Vice, one for each of the 7 Vices.<br /> <br /> Bunny - Lust<br /> <br /> Locust - Gluttony<br /> <br /> Badger - Pride<br /> <br /> Wolverine - Wrath<br /> <br /> Slug - Sloth<br /> <br /> Vulture - Envy<br /> <br /> Squirrel - Greed<br /> <br /> While it is true that the Beasts of Vice are not exactly allies with one another, they do share various traits in common. Their primary goal in the physical world is to spread their particular Vice, and in doing so they increase the power of their totem. By creating weaker spirits of their associated vice, their Totem is able to consume, fuse with, or bind these spirits into servitude. This results in increasing the Totem's rise through the ranks and subsequently affords the Beasts of Vice greater power as their Totem has more to spare.<br /> <br /> It is very rare for two different packs of the same breed to work together, their Totems are greedy for power and when they sense a rival of the same type as themselves, they tend to go to war with the rival Totem, rather than ally themselves with it. Despite this, there are cases of two or more packs absorbing each other following the results of a Totem war. The victorious spirit steals the power of the defeated spirit, and the losing pack joins the winning pack. Because of this, infighting is a fairly common occurrence for the Beasts of Vice.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ironically while their Totems may hate working with their own kind, they have occasionally been known to form truces with other Beasts of Vice. If the spirits can find some way to share their influence in the city, they may decide to use their packs to do so. However, these truces often end with bloodshed once the greedy Totem gets the upper hand, or feels like they aren't getting their fair share of Essence from the mortals they're preying upon.<br /> <br /> <br /> Because they can be created from ordinary humans who become susceptible to the Spirit's influence, Beasts of Vice can be of any ethnicity, be found in any geographical location, and can be descended from any culture. They aren't restricted to any exclusive heritage. This makes them notoriously difficult to locate for those who don't have the means to sense a person's true spiritual identity.<br /> <br /> <br /> ------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Life Span:</b> Many legends of shapeshifters peg them as being immortal creatures, but often these are exaggerations. While the Beasts of Vice are not actually immortal, they do have a reduced aging which allows them to live several times longer than ordinary humans. This can be either a blessing or a curse depending on which individual you ask. The rate at which they age varies between the breeds.<br /> <br /> <br /> Squirrel, Bunny, Locust = 1/3 normal rate. Max Life Span is early 300's<br /> <br /> Vulture, Badger, Wolverine = 1/5 normal rate. Max Life Span is early 500's<br /> <br /> Slug = 1/10 normal rate. Max Life Span is close to 1,000 years.<br /> <br /> This means that Squirrels, Bunnies, and Locusts can live 3 times longer than humans. Vultures, Badgers, and Wolverines can live 5 times longer. And Slugs can live 10 times longer!<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Unlike their enemies, the Uratha, Beasts of Vice are not part of an ancient culture passed down through heredity. Everyone of them was born as a human, but through events in their lives, they were changed into shapeshifters. There are two different ways this can happen, the first and most common is by having the changed forced upon them. The second method is by the human willingly agreeing to the change, usually by entering some kind of pact or bargain in exchange for power.<br /> <br /> <br /> When a Spirit of Vice reaches level 5 (minor god) rank in power, it can develop the ability to turn humans into Beasts of Vice, and usually takes on the role of acting as their Totem in the process. They seek out mortals who have a particularly strong connection to their Vice, as they are they are more susceptible to the spirit's influence and can thus be infused with the essence of the beast easier.<br /> <br /> Those who agreed to the change voluntarily are loyal servants to the spirit. They unite together in the form of cults who worship the Totem as some kind of deity and offer them essence as a sacrifice. Those who had the change forced upon them are treated like slaves, forced to carry out the spirit's will as they have neither the power nor the knowledge of how to fight back.<br /> <br /> <br /> Since Beasts of Vice are not part of some ancient lineage or bloodline, there is no danger when it comes to breeding among their own kind. Indeed, a Beast of Vice can mate with their own species as well as with full humans. Regardless of who they mate with, any child spawned will be born as a human, although it may exhibit a higher connection to the spirit world than normal.<br /> <br /> When a Beast of Vice mates with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance the child will exhibit a profound manifestation of the parent's Vice around the time puberty sets in. This risks increases to 60% for each child if they mate with their own species. These children attract the Totem's attention much more strongly than ordinary people, and are prime candidates to become a Beast of Vice themselves.<br /> <br /> For those who agreed to the change, this is a blessing, as they indoctrinate their children from an early age in service to the Totem, in a similar way one would teach a family religion. These viceful children risk drawing the attention of Child Services (especially those who display signs of Lust, Gluttony, or Wrath), and so the parents usually prefer to home school the children until they learn how to properly control themselves around others. They can't risk drawing attention from the government and exposing their operations both physical and spiritual.<br /> <br /> For those who had the change forced upon them, they usually abandon their children to what is presumably a better home, hoping that the spirits won't be able to find them, and thus spare them from the same fate as their parents. More often than not, these plans end up failing, as the slave fails to realize the true breadth of a spirit's perceptive abilities.<br /> <br /> <br /> Their method of reproduction also means that it is impossible to truly wipe out a Beast of Vice's pack as long as the Totem remains. You can slaughter every member of the pack, but as long as the Totem is still active, they can simply create some new replacements. The only way to truly stamp out the threat of them resurfacing is to destroy the totem itself. Even if a packmate or two survives the battle, they are incapable of producing more of their kind, and will become a much less immediate threat.<br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Gifts:</b> Just as there is no set background to what a Beast of Vice can be, there is also no set course of Gifts they can draw upon. Traditionally, Beasts of Vice are granted a Gift that enables them to invoke, control, or draw power from the vice they represent. Werebunnies harness lust, Werevultures harness envy, and so on.<br /> <br /> It is safe to say that they do not have access to gifts of Luna or Father Wolf for obvious reasons. However, they can obtain similar gifts from other malevolent spirits, such as gifts to amplify their physical attributes, manipulate the weather, and so on. Obtaining such powerful gifts is usually more difficult, but not entirely impossible. In most cases, only those who have proven themselves worthy of their Totem's favor are granted such powers. The failures and the slaves are restricted until they earn the privilege.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Forms:</b> Although they are born human, Beasts of Vice have a stronger affinity towards their animal forms than they do their original form. This is especially common for those who were forced to become a Beast of Vice against their will. Unable to come to terms with what has happened to them, they feel more comfortable remaining in their fully animal form unless they need to interact with others socially. In this manner, packs often keep them around as pets, sometimes even locking them in cages until they have a use for them.<br /> <br /> Those who willingly embraced the change are able to cope with it and remain in their human form more often, but their behavior starts to reflect the beast in both mannerisms and personality. It as if the darker aspects of the person have been amplified beyond what they were prior to the First Change. Like the Uratha, they too need to shift every few days, but this has less to do with finding balance and more to do with being able to relieve the stress of pretending to be human for too long. Conversely, those forced to change feel a compulsion to remind themselves they were once human, in order to prevent themselves from going completely insane under the torment they suffer at the hands of their own pack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-22-2014, 10:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T06:50:56-07:00","id":156456,"title":"","content":"<b>Rites and Ceremonies:</b> Among those who willingly agreed to the change, Beasts of Vice have been known to perform a variety of rituals in service to their Totem. The following is an explanation of the more prevalent among them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Rite of Blending</i> - This ritual is essential for a Werebeast's training in being able to blend in with human society. Unfortunately, it is also one of the most difficult to perform. The ritual takes a full day to perform, during which time the Beast of Vice is forbidden to indulge their vice. The purpose of the ritual is to teach self control and to not attract attention from their enemies. However, being ruled by their vices in such a way, it is also quite stressful. The Rite of Blending is performed annually, on the eve of the anniversary when they underwent their first change. It symbolizes the last day they were human, before they became a Beast of Vice.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Rite of Indulgence</i> - This ritual is the opposite to the previous one. Where the Rite of Blending was about controlling themselves, the Rite of Indulgence is all about losing control. The ritual is a full day in dedication to satisfying their vice, and amassing power for their Totem in the process. Each breed has their own methods in which they choose to perform the Rite of Indulgence, as each vice has different methods of being satisfied. The Rite of Indulgence is performed annually on the anniversary of their First Change, and is considered a celebration of the day they became Beasts of Vice.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Rite of Leashing</i> - Not everyone willingly agrees to serve their Totem master, and when that happens, submission must be forced upon them. This ritual is used to bind an unwilling Beast of Vice into service and involves a variety of profane rituals that one could only learn by worshiping an evil spirit. After the ritual's completion, the victim is incapable of bringing purposeful harm to the pack or their Totem, and is compelled to obey any order the pack gives them, so long as it does not conflict with an order given by a higher ranking pack member. Orders given by their Totem supersede all other orders. Performing the Rite of Leashing involves all members of the pack who willingly serve the Totem. They must each sacrifice 1 essence to take part in the ritual, but must also sacrifice 1 Willpower, as their collective will invades and dominates the mind of the subject. The Totem is also said to do they same, thereby binding the subject into the cult.<br /> <br /> Theoretically, the Rite of Leashing can be broken if the subject has more Willpower than what is sacrificed in the rite. However, very few humans possess that strong of a will.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Rite of Changing</i> - This ritual is a form of celebration and is practiced almost exclusively by those who's families have willingly joined the Totem and its pack. It is performed on the day of a subject's First Change and indoctrination into the pack. The Rite of Changing features a worship ceremony of pledging one's oath to the Spirit of Vice, and indulging one's Vice in fun and exciting ways. Such methods of indulgence can include brawls for Wrath, orgies for Lust, scavenger hunts for Greed, daring competitions for Pride, ignoring one's obligations for Sloth, grand feasts for Gluttony, and stealing from others for Envy. The Rite of Changing reaches its climax when the subject finally undergoes their First Change. If multiple newcomers are taking part, the ceremony ends when the last of them changes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Rite of Cursing</i> - This ritual is performed exclusively on those who refuse to willingly become Beasts of Vice. Although the pack itself does not have the ability to create Beasts of Vice, the Rite of Cursing does make things easier for their Totem to finish the process. The Rite of Cursing involves the subject often being abducted (after rejecting the Totem on its first visit) and brought before an altar that is imbued by essence. They are tied down upon the altar, while those who follow the path of the shaman use their gifts to crank up the desired Vice of the subject, opening their minds and bodies to the Totem's influence and allowing them to forcefully convert them into a Beast of Vice. Although the subject usually undergoes their First Change on top of the altar itself, it's infusion of essence renders the altar nearly indestructible so long as the Totem remains. This allows even Werewolverines to thrash and flail all they please without breaking free of their bonds.<br /> <br /> This ritual is usually followed up by the Rite of Leashing immediately afterward.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-02-2014, 09:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T08:15:01-07:00","id":156536,"title":"","content":"I've been working on a type of spider shifter that's based on the Japanese myth of the Spider Women, if anyone is interested. It's still a work in development, but I have the basics down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/427-rahuman101","name":"Rahuman101","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar427_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"95"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-04T08:20:21-07:00","id":159066,"title":"","content":"((Finally got around to finishing this. Also, I'm going to go back and tweak some of the information about the previous Beasts of Vice. Most of their fully animal forms are far too large to not induce Delusion in normal humans. I also forgot to mention those forced to become BoV prefer to remain in fully animal form as their default shape.))<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Beasts of Greed (Weresquirrels):</b> Before their First Change, Weresquirrels were hoarders, thieves, penny pinchers, and other life styles that invoke the desire to keep rather than to spend. This avarice brings them to the attention of a Spirit of Greed who initiates the first change either willingly or by forcing it upon them.<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change for a Beast of Greed is a risky one. They are filled with an insatiable urge to hoard something specific and pile it in the location in which the First Change takes place. Their urge is so powerful, they will actually go out and scour as much of the city as they can cover looking for any specimens and then hauling them back to their First Change site. By the time the First Change wears off, the Weresquirrel is likely to be surrounded by a heap of the object they were trying to collect and have little memory as to what made them think it was so important in the first place. Many of these specimens are likely stolen, and just like the Werevultures, they may need to go into hiding until the authorities lose their trail.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oddly enough, many Weresquirrels willingly accepted the change and they utilize their new found powers to become peerless thieves, burglars, and heist pullers. With their new squirrel-like agility, they can slip past defenses and scale surfaces no ordinary human could ever accomplish outside of a spy movie. Without utilizing high tech equipment, their handiwork is often so precise, that officials begin to suspect it might have been an inside job!<br /> <br /> <br /> Unlike most other shapeshifters, the Weresquirrels are NOT a pack based society. They embody greed, and the greedy do not like sharing what they have. When two or more Weresquirrels gather together, it is usually because they wish to breed, and then they separate afterwards. This makes the Weresquirrels vulnerable where numbers are concerned, but in terms of independence, they have more experience than other shifter breeds.<br /> <br /> <br /> Weresquirrel may not be physically overwhelming in a fight, but what they lack in strength they make up for with their agility. An experienced Weresquirrel has almost full dominance over the terrain as they can leap, dodge, pivot, climb, and dance around their opponent with great speed and precision. Their fighting style often involves wearing the enemy down with hit and run tactics, or getting them so frustrated they can't think straight.<br /> <br /> Although Weresquirrels do not have a society based on pack unison, they do recognize that sometimes there is safety in numbers. When multiple Weresquirrels are in the area, they have been known to form a truce in the face of a greater threat. These truces usually have a few ground rules.<br /> <br /> 1 - Keep away from my hoard.<br /> <br /> 2 - This is an alliance of convenience. I don't trust you, and you'd be stupid to trust me!<br /> <br /> 3 - Our enemies travel in groups, so if we're going to survive, we need to work together for now.<br /> <br /> Packs of Weresquirrels usually live a sizable distance away from each other. This is because just like regular squirrels, they have a tendency to steal from one another, which leads to infighting and territorial disputes. Often at times, these chaotic packs actually work for the same Totem, and their infighting provides it with greater power. When it comes to Weresquirrel packs, the one who is most favored by their Totem is usually the one with the largest hoard, which is another reason for their constant infighting as they try to prove themselves worthy of greater power and gifts.<br /> <br /> <br /> Weresquirrels are known to hoard essences like they were giant acorns! Their dens often have at least one room stacked to the ceiling with essence that is offered as a sacrifice to their Totem, or used to feed their own powers. Even when a Weresquirrel is encountered by themselves, both they and their Totem have a massive supply of spiritual energy to draw upon, which allows them to fight longer and harder than other shifters who gather essence in moderation. In fact, when there is a pack of Weresquirrels, their Totem's supply of essence can seem limitless to an unsuspecting Uratha!<br /> <br /> Like all Beasts of Vice, the Weresquirrels have extended lifespans beyond normal human capacity. They age at 1/3 the normal rate, physically aging by only 1 year for every 3 that passes by. An elder Weresquirrel can approach the early 300's in age, and during that time they often build up massive fortunes, usually by ill gotten means.<br /> <br /> <br /> -------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> Human: The human form of a Weresquirrel is typically the same as most other humans, but has various quirks that make them stand out. Their eyes and head are constantly shifting to observe their surroundings. As if they are either search for something, or expecting to get robbed at any moment. They move with an uncanny sense of balance or grace, and might be talented acrobats either professionally or recreational.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hybrid: Like the Werebunnies, the hybrid form of a Weresquirrel is surprisingly lithe and elegant in its design. They are more acrobatic than brutish and are typically faster than other shifters while in this form. The hybrid Weresquirrel is roughly the same size as their human form, although it loses excess fat and replaces it with athletic muscle tone. They gain the ability to use a claw attack, but their teeth are blunted and not effective at biting. One of its trademark characteristics is the bushy tail.<br /> <br /> <br /> Animal: In their fully animal form, the Beast of Greedy takes on the form of a squirrel. Although they are too small and weak to be much use in combat, they are incredibly fast and agile. This form is primarily used for scouting, infiltration, and making quick escapes.<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Human: In this form, the Beast of Greed's body shifts to become more nimbly built. Their body slims down and muscles strengthen to allow for advanced displays of acrobatics. Their nails extending into a pair of short (roughly 1/2 inch) claws and they might develop a pair of buck teeth.<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Animal: In this form, the Beast of Greed takes on the form of a giant squirrel, roughly the size of a human. Although it is not quite as fast as the fully animal form, it is much stronger and therefor better suited to combat.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Unless it is to unite against a common foe, the only other time Weresquirrels gather together is for breeding purposes. They mate, they conceive, and they seperate while the mother goes onto raise the child and the father returns to his hoard. Like all Beasts of Vice, the children are born human, but may develop a stronger affinity to the spirit world than normal.<br /> <br /> When a Weresquirrel mates with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance of the child inheriting a profound manifestation of their parent's vice. This chance increases to 60% for each child if they mate with another weresquirrel. These children are usually prime candidates to be targeted by their Totem and become another Weresquirrel when they are old enough.<br /> <br /> Because romance is pretty alien between two Weresquirrels, they have a mating ritual they perform when attempting to breed with their own kind. The Beasts of Greed hate surrendering anything freely, and for a female Weresquirrel, this includes their own body. The ritual involves the male having to chase down the desired female and is only allowed to mate with her if he can actually catch her. These chases can be lengthy processes as the Weresquirrels dash across roof tops, climb over fences, duck behind alleyways, and shift between their various forms to suit the situation. If they are witnessed conducting the ritual, many would mistake it as a parkour exercise, for it often requires incredible feats of athletic prowess and life risking jumps.<br /> <br /> There is no actual time limit to how long the ritual lasts, but if the male loses the female's trail, or becomes too tired to continue the chase, he is usually considered to have failed. To begin the ritual, the male simply has to declare his intentions (an open area is considered more honorable) to the desired female, and the chase begins. If he fails to catch her, he can not initiate another challenge for an entire month. Hopefully, he'll use that time to improve his poor athletic performance!<br /> <br /> To those who are not aware of the nature of this ritual, male Weresquirrels can very easily be mistaken for rapists, chasing a girl down and breeding her! As such, it is usually an unspoken curtesy that the ritual is not declared in public, where witnesses may get the wrong idea.<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Kleptomania - Those who witness a Beast of Greed in their hybrid, near animal, near human forms, or while shifting will be stricken with an extremely powerful urge to hoard whatever they can get their hands on. This urge is so powerful that those afflicted by it will stop paying attention to the Weresquirrel and begin scavenging the area for trinkets, baubles, or whatever junk catches their attention, piling it up into a heap. By the time their Kleptomania wares off, they will have forgotten about the Weresquirrel as they try to figure out what to do with the items they've collected.<br /> <br /> Rage: Death Rage - Weresquirrels guard their hoards with their lives, and will lash out at just about anyone who would dare steal from them.<br /> <br /> Primal Urge: Hoarding - Beasts of Greed are driven to hoard what they own, especially when it comes to essences. They do not like to spend, and the idea of charity is abhorrent to them. A Beast of Greed must spend 1 willpower to offer their services to others when there is no immediate reward to be gained from such assistance. Additionally, they must save against a Death Rage if they catch anyone trying to steal from them.<br /> <br /> Favored Renowns:<br /> <br /> Favored Gifts:<br /> <br /> <br /> Form Modifications:<br /> <br /> <br /> Human: None<br /> <br /> Animal: Size -2, Strength -3, Dexterity +3, Stamina +1, Speed +4, Perception +3<br /> <br /> Hybrid: Strength +1, Dexterity +3, Stamina +2, Size +1, Health +2, Initiative +1, Speed +5, Perception +3<br /> <br /> Near Human: Strength +1, Stamina +1, Health +2, Perception +1, Manipulation -1<br /> <br /> Near Animal: Size +1, Strength +1, Dexterity +2, Stamina +2, Speed +2, Perception +4","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-05T17:17:22-07:00","id":160393,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=142851#post142851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/15828-new-shifter-kushtaka-the-trickster-wereotters\">Wereotters who weave loci</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love these guys <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-05T17:27:44-07:00","id":160402,"title":"","content":"As for shapeshifters, I have created were-pangolins who act as healers and are living Loci, as well as a bizarre race of chimeric shapeshifters who were the spawn of an Idigam.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-22T18:57:46-07:00","id":177532,"title":"","content":"<b>Beasts of Gluttony (Werelocusts):</b> Before the First Change, Werelocusts are people who can't recognize that too much of a good thing is bad for you. While they fit the traditional ideal of Gluttony in that they are voracious eaters, they also fit its more abstract definitions. Drug addicts, party animals, thrill seekers, and even pain. Their insatiable appetites attracts spirits of gluttony who initiates the First Change either willingly or forcing it upon them.<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change for a Werelocust is a shocking and dangerous event. They develop a mind racking and insatiable craving for something like those mentioned above. During their First Change, they will indulge themselves to the point of nearly lethal intakes of such substances. And when the First Change wears off, their body needs to purge what it can not handle. They may spend hours vomiting up half digested food, or going into shock from an overdose. Their nerves may be temporarily wrecked from performing so many dangerous stunts, requiring them to regenerate before they can regain full mobility again. Even with their new regenerative powers, it takes a day for their body to recover from the experience. It is an agonizing day of healing and purging toxins from the body, and some do not survive the traumatic experience. Those who do, however, are now Werelocusts.<br /> <br /> <br /> Werelocusts are voracious creatures both in body and in spirit. Among all Shapeshifters they are one of the most cursed, for they burn through Essence at an accelerated rate. Every action the Werelocust invokes that uses Essence costs 3X the regular amount. As such, they are constantly hungry for more. If left unchecked, a plague of Werelocusts can devour all the essence in a Loci in a matter of days, leaving it as a spiritually dead zone! This makes them an extreme threat in the eyes of the Uratha and must be dealt with immediately before they turn the Shadow into a barren wasteland!<br /> <br /> <br /> Werelocust Totems use Essence in much the same way one would use a treat for taming a dangerous animal. They dangle essence in front of the Werelocust and tell them they will be rewarded for performing a service, which the shifter usually agrees to. However, they are hungry again mere hours later, and the Totem undoubtedly has yet another task for them to complete.<br /> <br /> The insatiable appetites of the Werelocusts give rise to new spirits of Gluttony which their totem can devour, merge with, or bind into service in order to increase its own power. Like all Beasts of Vice, they have extended lifespans compared to normal humans. They age at 1/3 the normal rate, physically aging by 1 year for every 3 that goes by. An elder Werelocust can reach the early 300's near the end of their lifespan.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> Human: Although appearing much the same way as their original human forms, a Werelocust's body starts to show the signs of excessive indulgence. Those who possess eating disorders are typically obese, while drug addicts have bodies that are shot halfway to hell with side effects and health conditions. Thrill seekers have scars across their body from a lifetime of constant injury and hospital visits. In all cases it becomes very clear that this person just doesn't know when to stop.<br /> <br /> <br /> Animal: Like their Wereslug cousins, the Werelocust does not change into a single animal, but actually becomes a whole swarm. In their fully animal form, the Werelocust dissolves into a swarm of 10 Locusts for each rank of spirit power they have achieved (maximum of 50 Locusts at rank 5). Their attributes and health boxes count for the entire swarm, not each individual locust. When all their health boxes are destroyed, the swarm is dead. However, as long as at least one locust survives, they can reform themselves. While in this form, the Werelocust gains the power to fly, greatly improving their mobility.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hybrid: In this form, the Werelocust takes the shape of an anthropomorphic insect with pincers erupting from their cheeks, wings growing from their backs, oversized eyes, antennae, and claw like hands and feet. However their most noticeable trait is the armored plated exoskeleton which provides them much greater protection than soft flesh. While in this form, the Werelocust's hands and feet lose several of their digits, replacing the five toes with two pronged talons, and sacrificing 2 fingers for 3 pronged claws including two large fingers and a thumb.<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Animal: In this form, the Werelocust takes on the shape of a gigantic locust roughly the size of their human form. They can fly in this form, and the vibrations of their wings produce a nearly deafening hum to those close by. Their body is covered in an armor-like carapace, increasing their defensive abilities, and they gain clawed appendages on their legs.<br /> <br /> <br /> Near Human: In this form, the Werelocust retains most of their human body, but gains a number of improvements that makes them better suited to combat. Their skin becomes glossy and harder than normal, but is not as heavily armored as their hybrid form. The nails on their fingers and toes also become sharp and barbed like the claws of an insect. Their eyes increase in size by roughly 40% and their voice has a buzzing echo when they speak.<br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Werelocusts are born as ordinary humans that were infused with the essence of a beast when they drew the attention of a Spirit of Gluttony. In this case, they were infused with the spirit of a locust, a creature who is infamous for its voracious appetite. Werelocusts also have the smallest population of out all the Beasts of Vice, because having a large number of them is extremely dangerous even for the very Totems who they serve.<br /> <br /> When a Werelocust produces a child with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance the child will inherit a profound manifestation of their parent's Vice. If they produce a child with another Werelocust, this risk increases to 60% for each child produced. Regardless of who they mate with, the child is born as a human, but may have a higher affinity to the spirit world than most. Children who manifest Gluttony in such a way are more vulnerable to the Totem's influence and are often targeted sometime around puberty to be granted the First Change.<br /> <br /> <br /> Werelocusts have an nontraditional sense of marriage, as they do not enjoy the idea of being restricted to one lover. As Beasts of Gluttony, they are all about brutal excess. They want as much as they are capable of getting their hands on, and to this extent, they often indulge in polygamous relationships. Often one male will claim several females as his own, and vice versa when a female decides to have several males.<br /> <br /> <br /> Werelocust females are typically rare, this causes the males to fight over her when one appears. Appeasing her with gifts and other displays in an effort to win her affections and earn the right to breed her. Because of this, female Werelocusts are typically considered to be higher in status than males, at least in the sense that they are showered with praise from sexually repressed males.<br /> <br /> <br /> Because they are born as humans, a Werelocust pack can never be truly destroyed so long as the Totem remains. Even if every living member of the pack is killed, the Totem can simply create some new replacements. The only way to truly stop the Werelocusts from creating more of themselves is to destroy the Totem itself.<br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Withdrawal - Those who witness a Werelocust in their Hybrid, Near Human, or Near Animal forms or witness it shapeshifting are stricken with a near crippling sense of withdrawal symptoms. Whatever activity, substance, or food is the source of their cravings, they begin to suffer physical and psychological torment as they need to satisfy their craving immediately. They will often try to flee the area in an effort to satisfy their addition, and will likely forget about the Werelocust in the process as they are too distracted by the pain to focus their attention on the giant mutant insect.<br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Voracious Rage - Just like a true locust, the Beasts of Gluttony are prone to devouring everything in sight, leaving nothing behind but a barren wasteland. When a Werelocust gives into their rage, they become a ravenous monster who attempts to consume all organic matter including people. Should an entire pack give into their rage, they can strip a victim down to the bones in seconds!<br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> Addiction - Every Beast of Gluttony feels the compulsion to indulge themselves beyond healthy levels. While this stereotypically manifests in the form of eating disorders, this does not always have to be the case. Drugs, thrill seeking, video games, anything that they could build up a physical or psychological addiction to is a possibility. A Beast of Gluttony must devote no less than 1 hour equal to 1/2 of their Primal Urge to satisfying their addition. If they fail to do so, they will lose 1 Willpower for each hour they missed, when can not be regained until they have satisfied themselves. If their willpower reaches 0 from such a method, they will immediately fly into a Voracious Rage that lasts double their appropriate quota.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> Human: None<br /> <br /> Animal: Dexterity +3, Stamina +1, Size -2, Initiative +1, Speed +3, Perception +4, Can fly at a rate equal to their running speed.<br /> <br /> Hybrid: Size +1, Strength +2, Dexterity -1, Stamina +2, Health +4, Initiative +1, Speed +2, Perception +2, Armor +5, Claw + Bite attacks inflict lethal damage<br /> <br /> Near Animal: Size +1, Strength +2, Dexterity -2, Manipulation -3, Health +3, Initiative +2, Speed +4, Perception +2, Jump +3, Can fly at a rate equal to their running speed, Armor +3. Opponents must make a fortitude save or be rendered temporarily deaf when the Werelocust is flying.<br /> <br /> Near Human: Strength +1, Stamina +2, Health +2, Perception +1, Manipulation -1, Armor +2, Unarmed attacks inflict lethal damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-22-2014, 10:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-26T15:40:39-07:00","id":181296,"title":"","content":"<b>Beast of Wrath (Werewolverine):</b> Before the first change, Werewolverines are people who are prone to violent outbursts with or without provocation. They are destructive, impulsive, and exactly what a Spirit of Wrath is looking for in a shock trooper. They decide to invoke the First Change either willingly or unwillingly and turn the mortal into a weapon against the hated Werewolves.<br /> <br /> The First Change for a Werewolverine is mockingly similar to that of a Werewolf. They are overcome with a sense of rage that drives them into a killing frenzy. By the time the First Change wears off, the entire setting around the Beast of Wrath has been demolished. Tables are snapped in half, doors are ripped off their frames, broken glass litters the floor, and blood is caked upon the walls from victims who are too mutilated to identify anymore.<br /> <br /> <br /> Indeed, it seems that the Werewolverine's entire purpose is to mock the Uratha, even their very names sound familiar. Whatever the Werewolves possess, the Werewolverine has in gross exaggeration. They are faster, stronger, and more destructive than The People have been recorded. Power, brutality, and most importantly rage are coursing through these savage shapeshifters. More than any other Beast of Vice, the Beasts of Wrath are the most prone to taking on the Werewolves directly and they actually come out victorious on many occasions!<br /> <br /> Though true Wolverines are smaller than true wolves, their savagery has allowed them to fight off creatures much larger than themselves, including wolves. The Spirit of Wrath hopes that by harnessing such an animal, they can effectively fight off the Werewolves from enforcing their tyranny upon the spirit. It is not uncommon to hear that a Werewolverine is the biggest threat out of all the Beasts of Vice, at least in terms of offensive power.<br /> <br /> <br /> Although a Werewolverine didn't exactly have the most self control when they were human, they become practically barbarians after their First Change. The slightest grievance can set them off, which makes it extordinarily difficult for them to blend in with human society anymore. Even if they do manage to retain their composure and not shift on the spot, their victims are usually left crippled from such an encounter.<br /> <br /> Much like the Werevultures, it takes a while for the Beasts of Wrath to amass power for their Totem. Initially, the Totem has to devour, fuse with, or bind spirits that are produced from the excess rage of their own servants. However, as the Werewolverines grow in power, they become more adept with igniting the hatred and destructive impulses of those around them, providing a much larger feast for their Totem.<br /> <br /> As with all Beasts of Vice, the Werewolverines have extended lifespans beyond the normal human capacity. Although they are not immortal, they age at 1/5 the normal rate, physically aging by 1 year for every 5 that goes by. This allows them to retain their youth and vigor for longer periods of time and near the end of their life spans they can approach 500 years of age.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> Human: In their human form, a Beast of Wrath often has the personality of someone who is looking for trouble. Many of them are built like warriors, having powerful frames and battle scars. Others are just angry punks dressing up like gang members and carrying illegal weapons.<br /> <br /> Animal: In their animal form, a Beast of Wrath takes the shape of a wolverine, a vicious little animal who is prone to violence.<br /> <br /> Hybrid: In their hybrid form, the Beast of Wrath remains largely close to their normal height, but they do become more built for combat. Their muscles bulk up, they grow claws and sharper teeth, and typically resemble a cross between a Wolverine and a human. This form also makes it virtually impossible for them to hold back their wrath.<br /> <br /> Near Human: In their near human form, the Werewolverine grows a set of fangs, claws, their muscle mass bulks up, and their natural body hair might grow about half an inch, but otherwise they retain their human appearance. A good shape for entering combat when they don't want to blow their cover with a hybrid form.<br /> <br /> Near Animal: In this form, the Beast of Wrath becomes a giant wolverine about the same size as their human form. As if a normal wolverine wasn't dangerous enough, an oversize one should send any rational minded enemy running!<br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Beasts of Wrath are not one for romance and even those who try it end up failing miserably when they lose control of their tempers. The majority of children who possess a Werewolverine parent are bastards who were conceived during a rape. As such, their childhood is often spent being an outlet for their human parent's frustration, which leaves the child emotionally traumatized and confused as they lose the ability to associate with others that does not involve violence.<br /> <br /> When a Beast of Wrath mates with a human, there is a 1 in 3 chance the child will develop a profound manifestation of their parent's Vice. This chance increases to 60% for each child if they mate with a fellow Werewolverine. These children become primary targets of their Totem to be added into the pack when they come of age.<br /> <br /> Mating between two Werewolverines is also a violent affair, but not for the same reason as mating with humans. The adrenaline and sexual tension between them causes their savagery to intensify. It is not uncommon for a pair of Werewolverines to shift into their near human form in the act of copulating, clawing and biting at each other in erratic fits. By the time they finish, both mates are often covered in wounds and exhausted from their primal session.<br /> <br /> <br /> For similar reasons to the Beasts of Lust, the Beasts of Wrath usually prefer to home school their children when they have the opportunity to do so. A violent and uncontrollable child draws the attention of child services and city workers who run the risk of blowing their cover. This also reduces the chance of anyone discovering their children suffering abuse when at home. Sadly, this home schooling does nothing to help ease their social impairment when they're finally allowed in public.<br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Delusion: Red Rage </b>- Ordinary people who witness a beast of Wrath in their near human, hybrid, or near animal forms, or who witness them shifting will become filled with a sense of hatred unlike any they have ever known. Their vision becomes hazy and red around the edges, their pulse quickens, and they feel a powerful desire to smash something. By the time their Delusion wears off, the victim snaps out of their berserk state having very little memory of what transpired. If questioned on the subject, they will likely say they blacked out or don't know what came over time.<br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Death Rage - Werewolverines have even less control over their anger than a proper Werewolf, and share their ability to enter into a killing frenzy when pushed too far.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> As servants of Wrath, the Werewolverine feels a compulsion to battle. They are greatly agitated whenever they allow an insult to pass and most proceed to bash the face of anyone who tries to challenge them. Whenever a Beast of Wrath refuses an opportunity to fight after being provoked, they lose 1 Willpower for doing so. If they lose all their Willpower, the Beast of Wrath snaps as they lose their patience and enters into a Death Rage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Human</b> - None<br /> <br /> <b>Near Human</b> - Strength +2, Size +1, Health +2, Stamina +1, Perception +1, Manipulation -2, Rage Save -1<br /> <br /> <b>Hybrid Form</b> - Strength +4, Size +1, Dexterity +2, Stamina +2, Health +4, Initiative +2, Speed +7, Perception +3, Rage Save -3<br /> <br /> <b>Animal Form</b> - Size -2, Dexterity +1, Stamina +1, Initiative +3, Speed +3, Perception +4, Rage Save -1<br /> <br /> <b>Near Animal Form</b> - Strength +2, Dexterity +2, Stamina +2, Manipulation -4, Health +3, Initiative +2, Speed +5, Perception +3, Rage Saves -2","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-26T15:45:11-07:00","id":181301,"title":"","content":"By the way, I went back and edited the Werebunny's Rage to something more appropriate. Just need to do the Werebadgers and the Beasts of Vice will finally be done!<br /> <br /> Aside from Renowns and Gifts, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T02:03:36-07:00","id":181774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=154613#post154613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Weredragons</b> - An ancient and powerful breed of shifter who trace their lineage all the way back to the early days of Atlantis. There is still much debate over whether they are true shapeshifters, or some ancient Legacy of Thyrsus who became especially versed in the spirit realm.<br /> <br /> <br /> What do you guys think? Plausible?</div> </div> </div> </div> Not really, Atlantis was edited out of reality so it never happened, having an origin in something that never happened is fine for mages, since the watchtowers exist in the supernal anyway, but I imagine were-dragons would be a bit more earthly in nature.<br /> <br /> But since dragons predated Atlantis anyway, I'd make the were-dragons similarly older than Atlantis too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T02:11:16-07:00","id":181780,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=137076#post137076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm, judging by your descriptions of them, it sounds like its possible for ordinary people to become shapeshifters instead of having to inherit it. If that's true, it gives me a great idea for a series of shifters who are based on the character Vices! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div> I noticed no one either confirmed your suspicion here.<br /> <br /> Yes, the origins of shapeshifters is individual to the race of shapeshifters. Some may be blessed ala the werewolves, some may be genetic, some may be cursed, some may be infected, and some may be created ritually. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T02:32:48-07:00","id":181784,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=181774#post181774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not really, Atlantis was edited out of reality so it never happened, having an origin in something that never happened is fine for mages, since the watchtowers exist in the supernal anyway, but I imagine were-dragons would be a bit more earthly in nature.<br /> <br /> But since dragons predated Atlantis anyway, I'd make the were-dragons similarly older than Atlantis too.</div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking of that... that's another project I need to get working on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T03:52:52-07:00","id":181814,"title":"","content":"I like these shifters, but i must ask: was it really neccesary to make almost all werewolverines rape bastard childs?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T10:26:06-07:00","id":182091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=181814#post181814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like these shifters, but i must ask: was it really neccesary to make almost all werewolverines rape bastard childs?</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you imagine a servant of wrath to be the romantic sort for any period longer than a year?<br /> <br /> <br /> Also, thank you for the confirmation. ^_^","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-27-2014, 03:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-28T04:42:08-07:00","id":182792,"title":"","content":"Well, no. But there's a line between, aggresive, angry sex (wich is awesome) and rape. Maybe werewolverines are very much into bdsm as it may give them a controlled outlet for their passion. I mean, they may be wife beatters, wich is already horrible enough to fit WoD, without needing to get into the cheap shock trick of rape.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-24T15:49:36-08:00","id":321123,"title":"","content":"<b>I don't know if Skinwalkers exist yet (knowing my luck, they probably do), but here's my take on them!</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Skinwalkers (Weredogs):</b> In ancient times, When Luna was still grieving for the loss of her mate and had not forgiven the Forsaken, some of her children were still arrogant in what their progenitors had done. They saw Luna's wrath as too extreme, and sought ways to undo her punishment that bound them to human forms. It is unknown how long they roamed the world in those ancient times, questioning spirits and shaman alike. Eventually, however, their hubris obtained a result, and they restored their bestial powers.<br /> <br /> But a spirit as powerful as Luna would not be denied so easily it seemed, and their victory was a shallow one. Their canine forms were genetically inferior to their original state. Smaller, less organized, weaker, their claws were duller and their instincts diminished. These were the first generation of Skinwalkers, or as you might know them today as Weredogs.<br /> <br /> The Weredogs are pariahs among the Lupines who openly mock and deride them. In the eyes of the Uratha, a Skinwalker is a mutt, a mixed breed who's lineage has been diluted by the trappings of human civilization, such as greed and luxury. A dog is a pet, a domestic animal who can not survive long in the wilds as they grow soft from the affections of humanity. They have forgotten what it means to be a Wolf.<br /> <br /> Unlike their cousins who seek a balance among the physical and the spiritual, Weredogs are primarily concerned with the well being of humanity. They have become too indoctrinated into human life styles to fully return to the wilderness. So as long as humanity continues to prosper and expand, the Skinwalkers likewise are able to expand their own territory. They pay little heed to the concerns of the wildlife unless it negatively impacts their own well being, which places their reputation among the spirit world in an even worse position than their Lupine cousins.<br /> <br /> <br /> Being socially outcast from typical Werewolf society, the modern day Skinwalkers know relatively little about how the spirit world works. What they have managed to learn was passed down through many generations, or learned the hard way through personal experiences. This is another reason why the Wolves consider them inferior, as they often try to get involved in situations they know little about. They get even more flak when it comes to the Spirits. If the Spirits weren't happy with the Wolves forcing their rules upon everything, they are even less pleased about their bastardized cousins trying the same trick! Skinwalkers who choose to commune with the spirits have to work extra hard to earn their gifts and fetishes as they struggle to prove themselves worthy. Because they are so engrossed in human lifestyle, they typically form alliances with spirits that represent such aspects.<br /> <br /> <br /> Skinwalker society has two distinct sub-groups which are Pedigrees, and Mutts. A Pedigree Skinwalker is a Weredog who's parents and grandparents were both Skinwalkers. A Mutt is either someone who had a human ancestor within the last 2 generations, or someone who became infected, the process behind which shall be explained later. Pedigree Skinwalkers are considered to be higher in rank among their people, while Mutts are considered to be the inferior mixed bloods. It is a common sight to find Mutts acting as servants to Pedigrees when dealing with a Skinwalker pack.<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b><br /> <br /> Human: The human form of a Skinwalker looks almost completely identical to an ordinary human. There are no outstanding marks or characteristics that would identify them as a shifter. This is both to remind and mock them for how closely they have been corrupted by human influence.<br /> <br /> Animal: In their animal form, the Skinwalker takes on the shape of a dog who's species is randomly chosen upon undergoing the first change, unless they are the child of a Skinwalker, in which they will become the same species. The available dog species are large and medium sized breeds (Such as Dalmatians and Collies), but not the small breeds (such as Chihuahuas or Shih Tzu).<br /> <br /> Hybrid: In their hybrid form, the Skinwalker becomes a smaller and weaker mimicry to a Werewolf. They do not stand 9 feet tall and weigh 500 lbs of muscle, but they are certainly stronger than ordinary humans. The average size for the hybrid form is between 6 to 7 feet tall, with a weight close to 250 lbs.<br /> <br /> Near Human: In this form, the Skinwalker gains a set of claws and their mouth protrudes into a short muzzle, giving them enough room for their sharp, predatory teeth. Their body hair thickens, but they don't grow extra hair where it wasn't already present. Their muscles also bulk up slightly, making them stronger than their normal human forms. Clearly looking at this hairy, muscular thing would alert others to it's unnatural existence.<br /> <br /> Near Animal: This form is a much larger version of their animal form. In fact, it's roughly the size of a common wolf, making them easily recognizable as an unnatural member of their breed. It is unknown whether the size of this form is a curse, forever reminding them of what they used to be.<br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> There are two methods that Skinwalkers are able to reproduce. The first method is through mating, which is considered to be more honorable. Children born from such a union have a 1 in 3 chance to inherit the Skinwalker genes and undergo their first change. The second method is through infection, and it is considered dishonorable, for it depicts the Skinwalkers as rabid dogs. Spreading a disease to those they come into contact with, as opposed to an ancient and noble bloodline.<br /> <br /> The infection is transmitted with biting, not unlike rabies. Normal humans who receive the bite have a chance to become infected if the wound is not treated properly. If the infection takes hold, they will undergo the first change within one lunar cycle. The time it takes for the infection to set in depends on the individual, but rarely does it require more than a day of neglect to take hold.<br /> <br /> Only natural born Skinwalkers are considered pure bloods among their people. Those who become infected are considered mixed breeds and regarded as second class citizens by the rest of the Skinwalkers. Even then, only those who's ancestry spans at least 2 generations of Skinwalker couples is a true pure blood. Both their parents and both their grandparents must be Skinwalkers to be officially recognized as pure bloods. Anything less and they are a mixed breed in the eyes of their people.<br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> Lunacy - Skinwalkers may no longer be Werewolves, but they still retain the protection of Lunacy for those who witness them in their hybrid, near animal, or near human forms. Witnesses may interpret what they saw as a large dog, or a hallucination brought upon by the adrenaline rushing through their system.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b> Cunning - Having lost their primal instincts, the Skinwalkers had to rely on more inventive ways to survive throughout the ages.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Gifts:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Cunning</i> - Skinwalkers are not as strong or terrifying as their ancient counterparts. However, what they lack in savagery, they have made up for with their cleverness. Ages of living alongside humanity has imparted them with greater intelligence they can use to outwit foes they can not overpower.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>City</i> - Skinwalkers are often poorly equipped to survive in the wilderness, and prefer life in the city. As a result, they learned how to draw upon the Gifts it has to offer and further protect their homes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Wolfslayer</i> - When humans learned how to domesticate canines, they often used them as protection against wolves. It is no great surprise, then, that the Skinwalkers learned the secrets to battling against their Uratha cousins who would seek to impede human civilization.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Mad Rage - The corruption that twisted their animal forms also seems to have twisted their rage in the process. When a Skinwalker enters a Rage, they are like a rabid dog. Their teeth bare fiendishly, they emit gutteral snarls and barks, and their saliva begins to foam around the mouth. The Skinwalker is so driven by hatred and anger, they will even turn to acts of self inflicted injury hoping to burn off some of their pent up aggression. A Skinwalker's rage is just as dangerous to their own body as it is to everyone else around them, and therefor is even more terrifying of a concept to them.<br /> <br /> While in the Mad Rage state, the Skinwalker must roll a Composure save to avoid attacking their own body (tearing at their flesh or slamming against the environment) each round. This only applies if there is nobody else around in which to vent their rage on. The Skinwalker must be able to reach the potential victim as well, meaning if they are behind layers of safety glass, the Skinwalker would still attack themselves if they couldn't smash through.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> The Skinwalkers are able to survive because of human civilization and advancements. Because of this, the Skinwalkers feel compelled to aid them in order to facilitate their own survival in the process. Additionally, a Skinwalker can not bring purposeful harm to a human being unless one of the following is met<br /> <br /> 1 - The human is attacking another human ally of the Skinwalker. Much like a pet coming to protect its master from an attacker.<br /> <br /> 2 - The human attacks first and is not considered to be an ally. Standing alliances can be broken if the human continuously abuses the Skinwalker, thus proving that they aren't concerned with the Skinwalker's survival.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> Human - None<br /> <br /> Near Human - Speed +2, Strength +1, Dexterity +1, Health +1<br /> <br /> Hybrid Form - Size +2, Speed +3, Strength +2, Dexterity +2, Health +2, Perception +1<br /> <br /> Animal Form - Size -1, Speed +4, Dexterity +1, Strength +1, Perception +3<br /> <br /> Near Animal Form - Size +1, Speed +3, Strength +1, Dexterity +1, Health +1, Perception +2","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-24-2014, 11:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-24T18:36:26-08:00","id":321242,"title":"","content":"Reminds me of the wolfen, except they were just another species. I think Dungeons and Dragons would call them wolfweres.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-24-2014, 09:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-24T19:11:22-08:00","id":321283,"title":"","content":"Have some ideas, though I don't have War Against the Pure, and thus cannot stat them up. Several are related to Death. For example(inspired, as much as I'm ashamed to admit it, the Swan Maidens of Changing Breeds) who act as Psychopomps for the dead(I'm think they manifest as Swans if female and Ravens if male, though that's just a thought). Another idea are Owl-Shifter necromancers(in both senses of the word) with a strong hatred of the Strix and all derived from them(including the Kindred). <br /> <br /> I'm also considering some &quot;allied&quot; Shifters, the idea being their progenitors worked alongside Father Wolf, and so they have compatible, if not the same, goals. One group, the Girtablilu, are Were-Scorpions born of a giant, scorpion shaped spirit of boundaries and warding, and have dedicated themselves to guarding and controlling the places that link here and there. They have Auspice equivalents based on what type of gate they are most attuned to(for example, a Were-Scorpion who bears a &quot;avernian auspice&quot;(for lack of a better phrase), would be associated with gifts that better help dealing with the dead. They view the children of Father Wolf as &quot;childish&quot; for their &quot;fixation&quot; on the spirit realm.<br /> The other are the Rokea, the Were-sharks. They are the answer to the fact the Forsaken are, if not terrible swimmers, then heavily disadvantaged in water. They lack the taboo or threat associated with breeding amongst their own(though they still find it unspeakable to mate with a mundane shark), which is very well, considering they spent a lot of time far away from any humans. They are, unfortunately, far too much like Werewolves to comfortably exist alongside them, so the groups prefer no to interact, though in costal regions, the two groups have often cone to understandings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-24T20:39:04-08:00","id":321349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=321242#post321242\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Reminds me of the wolfen, except they were just another species. I think Dungeons and Dragons would call them wolfweres.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, Wolfweres were a reversed Lycanthrope. They spent their time as wolves, but turned into men during the full moon. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> I've also been working on Wererats who are the offspring of the Plague King before he was destroyed by Father Wolf. And I still haven't gotten around to adding the final <b>Beast of Vice</b> yet (Werebadgers - Pride).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-24-2014, 11:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-25T13:37:23-08:00","id":322120,"title":"","content":"Another Shifter idea. I'm not the most well read on Werewolf, but doesn't Luna have a brother, Helios, who hates the children of Father Wolf? Maybe he created his own breed of Shifters, a group of regal Were-Sacrabs, with their own &quot;auspices&quot; and Solim(fairly certain that that's not the right name) based on the three aspects of Ra and Dawn, Noon(know to them as Zenith) and Dusk(called by them Twilight). I'd suggest adding a Night group, but that'd be stretching the Exalted joke a bit too far, mostly because there isn't a evening based aspect of Ra. Hoping to not make them antagonists(or at least, not making them villains).<br /> <br /> EDIT: On the other hand, Sobek was sometimes worshiped as an aspect of Ra, in the form of a protection deity, and Ra was most vulnerable at night... So maybe the Night &quot;Auspice&quot; would be the Rahu equivalent?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3514,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","user":"ZealousChristian24","date":".  New Ideas 11-25-2014, 09:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-25T14:53:56-08:00","id":322214,"title":"","content":"Werescarabs would be terrifying beyond belief. Starship Trooper that shit!<br /> <br /> <br /> On another note, I amused myself with the idea of <b>Werecrabs</b> some time ago, who's mission in life is to protect the shorelines against spirits of the deep who might try to come onto dry land. They'd need that exoskeleton armor for the threats they face.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-25T15:57:03-08:00","id":322244,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=322214#post322214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Werescarabs would be terrifying beyond belief. Starship Trooper that shit!<br /> <br /> <br /> On another note, I amused myself with the idea of <b>Werecrabs</b> some time ago, who's mission in life is to protect the shorelines against spirits of the deep who might try to come onto dry land. They'd need that exoskeleton armor for the threats they face.</div> </div> </div> </div> This needs to be a thing. This very much needs to be a thing.<br /> EDIT: As for their &quot;Auspices&quot;, maybe;<br /> \u2022HIGH-TIDE: When They-Who-Lurk-Below strike, it is they of the High-Tide who stand in their way, strong hearted warriors who even Death fears to claim.<br /> \u2022LOW-TIDE: When the tide recedes, the battle pauses, and the eye of the storm descends, it is the thinkers and sages of the Low-Tide who take advantage. With their cunning and insight, they plan and scheme to exploit the home advantage.<br /> \u2022MIX-TIDE: In and out, high and low. When the ebb follows the flow, balance has been achieved, something best understood by those of the Mix-Tide. Leaders and diplomats, they put others where they are best needed, and they are the ones who grit their teeth,sacrifice their honor and call for help when they are in need of aid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3514,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","user":"ZealousChristian24","date":".  New idea 11-25-2014, 09:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-25T16:41:41-08:00","id":322262,"title":"","content":"I created a group of Weredogs for a game set in Detroit (I based it around the idea that they have a big problem with packs of wild stray dogs roaming the abandoned parts of the city). Only one shifter was actually born that way, who would be the alpha, and they could infect others through biting. This was incredibly stressful on the infected though so I had most of the them end up with permanent derangements. The pack would prey on the homeless to increase their ranks. I didn't get to develop them too much as the game fell apart.<br /> <br /> EDIT, more than one person can be born as a pack alpha but they don't stick together so you'll only find one natural shifter per pack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1279-badger-king","name":"Badger King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1279_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T10:41:00-08:00","id":323742,"title":"","content":"Good News, everyone! I found a website that gave somewhat better instructions on creating new breeds. So I should have better fleshed out versions from now on. I also found out there already exists Wererats. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.thesubnet.com/portal/wod2/wtf/Shapechanger.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.thesubnet.com/portal/wod2/wtf/Shapechanger.html\" target=\"_blank\"> 401 Unauthorized </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.thesubnet.com/portal/wod2/wtf/Shapechanger.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.thesubnet.com/portal/wod2/wtf/Shapechanger.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> ------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Sahuagin (Sa-HWAH-gin):</b> Anyone who knows anything about the Amazon jungles and rainforests agrees that it is an exceptionally dangerous environment. Those who do not have a proper guide are more than likely to end up dead within the first 24 hours of exploration. Few outsiders, however, know the reason behind such dangers. The Sahuagin are the piranha bloods of the jungle, and they are extremely territorial.<br /> <br /> Keeping primarily to the water such as rivers and oasis, the Sahuagin dislike the presence of foreigners who have a long history of chopping down their forests and hunting the beasts for sport. Over the centuries, they have watched as the tribesmen attempted to negotiate with these foreigners about the sanctity of the wildlife, but to no avail. The Sahuagin are tired of negotiations, the time has come for them to act! Foreigners who do not follow a guide's instructions are likely to be snatched up by surprise, their screams muffled as they are dragged to the water and torn to pieces by these vengeful shifters.<br /> <br /> Because they are indigenous to the Amazon, Sahuagin culture is very tribal. They live deep in the jungles, away from the prying eyes of mortals. Like the beasts they resemble, Sahuagin are blood thirsty and savage, natural born hunters and skilled warriors. Traditionally, their weapon of choice is the spear, for its hydrodynamic shape makes it an ideal choice for their amphibious fighting style.<br /> <br /> To protect the Amazon and safeguard the exotic wildlife that resides there, that is the Accord that the Sahuagin pledge themselves to. If the white men from across the sea will not listen to warning, the Sahuagin will make an example of them. That being said, the Sahuagin need to eat too, but when they hunt the animals of the jungle, they do so because they need resources, not because they want bragging rights or trophies.<br /> <br /> ------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Appearance:</b> Being native to the Amazon, the Sahuagin are typically dark skinned. The hostile environment they live in has also conditioned them with athletic builds, either for chasing down prey, or fleeing a more dangerous hunter. Those who manage to catch a view of their human forms (before they launch into attack mode) might notice that their eyes are unusually large for a human's. Their pupils dialated and glossy like those of a fish. While in human form, they can only survive on land, for they are restricted to breathing air.<br /> <br /> <br /> In War Beast form, the Sahuagin resembles the creature from the black lagoon. A monstrous hybrid of man and fish, their mouth is nearly as wide as their face and filled with sharp teeth. Their digits become webbed for easier swimming, and a ridge of fins grows around their face. The warform can survive both on land and underwater thanks to having both a pair of lungs and set of gills, and their rudder-like tail makes it easier to maneuver in the water. While in this form, Sahuagin rarely exceed 140 lbs. But it's not the size that makes them dangerous, it's their mobility.<br /> <br /> In their animal form, the Sahuagin is an abnormally large piranha, weighing an estimate 50 lbs. A school of them working together can strip a man to the bone in seconds. While in this form, the Sahuagin can not breathe air, and loses the ability to use any human instruments because of the fact they are a giant fish. This form is confined to the water, which means it has a limited range of uses. It is primarily used as the patrol form of the Sahuagin, as they swim undetected through the water ways, keeping a look out for trouble.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> Intensely xenophobic, the Sahuagin primarily mate with natives of the Amazon, commonly members of other tribes they have formed alliances with. Legends state that children who are born in the water are blessed by the spirits who reside there. By tradition, Sahuagin mothers who are ready to birth will enter into a pool of water that is at least deep enough to reach their hips. The child is baptized in the waters of the jungle the moment they enter the world, before being allowed to take their first breath of air.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Delusion:</b> The Sahuagin do not have a delusional protection. They do not need it, for those who witness them rarely survive the encounter. Those who do manage to escape, however, are not likely to be taken seriously when they recount the story to others. Ever since white men first discovered the rainforest, it has been attributed to ridiculous legends and fairy tales. Survivors are likely to be mocked for having been exposed to some kind of exotic drug, or watching too much Indiana Jones.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Renowns:</b> Purity - Outsiders bring foolishness and destruction. That is what the Sahuagin have come to realize over the centuries. White men have no respect for their home, the animals, the people, or the spirits who live there until such a time as they are near the brink of extinction. If the Amazon is to be protected, it must be cleansed of outsider presence. Sahuagin do not negotiate, they do not inquire, they do not try to rationalize. They are warriors, and these are foreigners invading their home.<br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Sahuagin live in a place where everything wants to kill you if you aren't careful. Growing up in that kind of environment can really give somebody a violent streak, but when you grow up as half beast, it produces something wholly terrifying. When a Sahuagin enters a rage, they do not qualify as human, they are a denizen of the jungle, a beast who has learned to survive despite the odds, and now it is pissed off!<br /> <br /> <b>Primal Urge:</b> Sahuagin are drawn to the water, and do not enjoy straying too far away from it. Fortunately for them, they live in the rainforest where showers are plentiful. When water falls from the sky, the Sahuagin feel more comfortable traveling abroad. During the monsoon seasons, the Sahuagin tend to become nomadic, traveling great distances across the Amazon that would otherwise be impossible for them.<br /> <br /> <br /> -------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Form Modifications:</b><br /> <br /> Hybrid - Strength +3, Stamina +3, Manipulation -2, Size 6, Health +4, Speed +4, +1 perception rolls.<br /> <br /> Animal - Strength +1, Stamina +2, Manipulation -2, Size 2, Health -2, Speed +6 (0 on Land), +2 Perception rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T11:04:54-08:00","id":323767,"title":"","content":"Yeah, um, you probably shouldn't consult that. It appears to be Changing Breeds rules, and from what I've heard, you should avoid those like they are on fire and actively planning on eating your children.<br /> <br /> Anyway, to contribute to the thread, I'd like to share something I call, for lack of a better name, a &quot;good merge&quot;. It would be a local(and probably relatively recent) phenomena. The idea is that Hosts are, for reason even they don't understand, drawn to the city from miles away. They will, of course, attempt to gain a host body. Except not only can they not do what they do(Spider's can't eat the brain, Rats can't eat through the ribs), but they find themselves returned to the immaterial and promptly devoured by the soul of their would-be-host, who, in immense pain and heavily freaked out and confused, promptly undergoes the First Change. They have many of the same abilities of the Forsaken, abet with different forms and none of the culture... Or prescriptions to stay quiet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T12:37:48-08:00","id":323821,"title":"","content":"Hmm, well the site says you have to be born a shifter, yet when I asked around, people said there were other methods to become one too, including biting. So I'm guessing the other books provided contradictions to the Changing Breeds version? I tried looking for a video about it, and from what I was able to understand, Changing Breeds placed emphasis on the idea that humanity was evil, and nature was good. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-27-2014, 03:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T15:30:01-08:00","id":323895,"title":"","content":"Less contradict as completly ignore for the sake of human sanity and dignity everywhere, as I understand it.( Here's a collection of posts from Something Awful dedicated towards reviewing it: <a href=\"http://destroythis.blogspot.com/search?updated-max=2014-07-31T18:46:00-07:00&amp;max-results=7&amp;start=7&amp;by-date=false&amp;m=1\" target=\"_blank\">http://destroythis.blogspot.com/sear...date=false&amp;m=1</a>) As far as I'm aware, the &quot;right&quot; Shifter creation rules are in the final chapter of War Against the Pure, titled Wayward Sons. I've heard it constantly praised in the same breath people use to bash Breeds, so there's that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T18:44:53-08:00","id":324037,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=ZealousChristian24;n323767}<br /> <br /> Anyway, to contribute to the thread, I'd like to share something I call, for lack of a better name, a &quot;good merge&quot;. It would be a local(and probably relatively recent) phenomena. The idea is that Hosts are, for reason even they don't understand, drawn to the city from miles away. They will, of course, attempt to gain a host body. Except not only can they not do what they do(Spider's can't eat the brain, Rats can't eat through the ribs), but they find themselves returned to the immaterial and promptly devoured by the soul of their would-be-host, who, in immense pain and heavily freaked out and confused, promptly undergoes the First Change. They have many of the same abilities of the Forsaken, abet with different forms and none of the culture... Or prescriptions to stay quiet.[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> I personally would make it something about the host's would be victim that causes it. Maybe mortals that were blessed by Luna (for whatever mad reason of hers) have the same thing that separates the werewolves from the hosts, but without the animal spirit half that let's them shapeshift and stuff. Therefore, when a host tries to merge with them, they fail and get absorbed.<br /> <br /> Of course, this means that these weres probably have auspices, but may not have the exact same purpose in Luna's plan as the forsaken.<br /> <br /> But that's just how I would do that idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T18:58:03-08:00","id":324042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=323895#post323895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Less contradict as completly ignore for the sake of human sanity and dignity everywhere, as I understand it.( Here's a collection of posts from Something Awful dedicated towards reviewing it: <a href=\"http://destroythis.blogspot.com/search?updated-max=2014-07-31T18:46:00-07:00&amp;max-results=7&amp;start=7&amp;by-date=false&amp;m=1\" target=\"_blank\">http://destroythis.blogspot.com/sear...date=false&amp;m=1</a>) As far as I'm aware, the &quot;right&quot; Shifter creation rules are in the final chapter of War Against the Pure, titled Wayward Sons. I've heard it constantly praised in the same breath people use to bash Breeds, so there's that.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> &quot;- Changing Breeds have always existed.<br /> - Tacked-on Lovecraft reference to Great Old Ones possibly existing.<br /> - The First were red in tooth and claw, hunting everything, life was good, etc etc Garden of Eden myth that was done infinitely better in Werewolf. I'm going to be saying that last part a lot, if you haven't noticed.<br /> - Everything was relatives and one, flesh and spirit mixed, and everything was fuckin' each other. No, dead serious, that's one of the 'possibilities' they mention except they expound upon it for a giant paragraph so you know which way they lean. The Snake was bangin' Eve, women were all up on the beasts, lost gods were bangin' mankind to make Nephilim, nobody could keep it in their pants.<br /> - Nature with a capital N is alive and a thing. Totally not Gaia.<br /> - Human intelligence is heavily implied to be evil.<br /> - The Clever Monkey becomes Man the Conqueror and starts fucking Nature sideways. Not like that you weirdos.<br /> - Changing Breeds could not be enslaved so they were cast out and killed.<br /> - Religion is made, used to conquer natural world of spirits.<br /> - Industry overtakes religion, now Man is The Despoiler.<br /> - Nature has arbitrarily decided it's time to actually fight back against Man for some reason instead of slapping its shit when the Industrial Revolution started, Changing Breeds begin campaign of ecoterrorism.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Well I like the book so far. :P</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> Anyways....<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Kraken:</b> Squid and Octopi shifters, capable of taking on the colossal sized beast forms of which they are named.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Hook Tongues:</b> Leech shifters who reside in swamps and murky environments.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Riot Ragers:</b> Bees, Hornets, and Wasps that descend upon their enemies in homicidal war bands.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Prickle Furs:</b> Porcupine, Hedgehogs, and Echidna, their backsides are nearly as dangerous as attacking them head on!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-28-2014, 02:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-27T21:32:22-08:00","id":324204,"title":"","content":"Ah, okay, read a few more pages of the rant. The story elements are interesting, but apparently the game play mechanics were FUBAR.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-28T02:26:21-08:00","id":324322,"title":"","content":"I would say the story elements were messy as well. It was like Apocalypse all over again, humanity is eeeeeevil, nature will teach them a lesson! So on and so forth. So black and white it looked like an mude film.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-28T05:59:17-08:00","id":324426,"title":"","content":"Well I can kind of understand that type of story. Weres are connected with the spirit world, after all. And from a spirit's perspective, humanity could be a bunch of self entitled asses. Makes sense when you remember that half your mind is influenced by animals, rather than humanity.<br /> <br /> I was going to write up the Weredragons last night, but it was 2 in the morning, and I was too tired. I'll get to it later on today. I was thinking of making them agents of justice, delivering punishment to the evil and blessings to the good. Kind of like the stories of dragons disguising themselves as beggars and asking for shelter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-28-2014, 09:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-28T08:08:07-08:00","id":324530,"title":"","content":"Sounds interesting!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-28T12:10:07-08:00","id":324714,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\">Alright, here's what I came up with. I didn't include form modifications, because I couldn't think of them. But the basic information on them was done.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Noble Wyrms:</b> There are none still living who remember the origins of these ancient and powerful beings. Carrying the blood of dragons within them, their true kin appear to have left this world long ago. If traces of their progenitors remain on earth, they have not been discovered by archeologists yet.<br /> <br /> The appointed role of the Noble Wyrms is to be judges over the world. To those who are found worthy, they deliver the blessings of the heavens upon them. To those who are unworthy, they face retribution for their crimes. Over the centuries, these have given rise to stories of seemingly helpless beggars and traveling salesmen asking for shelter or peddling their wares. Those who treat them generously are granted a reward for their kind hearts. Those who abuse them are punished severely for their lack of mercy. In recent memory, the Noble Wyrm's have had to lay down retribution less often than they did in the medieval era. The existence of soup kitchens, charity drives, peace corp. and other good will organizations has provided help to those in need when such needy people would be treated as third class citizens in ages gone by. The Noble Wyrms view this with hope that their work has begun to pay off, and with further guidance from them, it will get better over time.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Noble Wyrms are a collection of breeds from around the world, primarily involving those of Aisan and European folk lore. Among the shifter community, there are tales of lesser reptiles who bow in reverence to the Noble Wyrms, attributing them to the ranks of nobility and perhaps even godhood. Another note of their regalia is the fact that they are prone to vast hordes of wealth and knowledge. Noble Wyrms can have entire rooms of their household dedicated to stock piling volumes of books, or treasured artifacts. These artifacts are usually granted to those who are found worthy of reward, but anyone who attempts to steal them will face the dragon's wrath!<br /> <br /> <br /> Noble Wyrms have incredibly long life spans. If they are allowed to live in peace and maintain their health, they can live for thousands of years. The cause behind this lifespan is probably due to the extremely low fertility rate the Noble Wyms possess, but we will get to that momentarily. Suffice it to say, Noble Wyrms are one of the rarest breeds of shifters on the planet.<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Reproduction:</b> As just mentioned previously, Noble Wyms do not have a very high birth rate. Although not in the way you may be thinking. During their great life span, a Noble Wyrm can have dozens, possibly even hundreds of children depending on their sexual activity. However, in all that time, only about seven offspring will undergo The Change and become Noble Wyms. By the time they finally do produce a 7th Weredragon, they are too old to have anymore children, or merely lose interest in trying.<br /> <br /> Because of it's rarity to manifest, Noble Wyms are extremely protective of their children who do undergo their First Change. Until the child is strong or smart enough to take care of themselves, their parents will defend them to the death if need be.<br /> <br /> When the child grows mature enough, they usually leave for new lands in search of potential mates. Probably because half the families within a 100 mile radius are distant relatives of theirs, and that would be incest. It may very well be impossible to tell how many families today carry dragon blood in their lineage.<br /> <br /> <br /> Another thing that should be noted is that Noble Wyms tend to have very specific kinks when it comes to breeding. Some of these are merely eccentric and others are life threatening. The stories of dragons who only take virgins to their bed is one of the most well known. Other stories tell of Noble Wyms who mate during free fall, their bodies plummeting to the ground as they reach climax. The strange or extreme nature of these mating rituals is likely another cause behind their low fertility rate, as finding a willing partner can be a bit of a chore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>First Change: </b>The first change happens differently for Asian and European dragons. In Asia, the First Change is triggered by means of obtaining great wisdom, often following an epiphany of sorts, or perhaps they obtain enlightenment at a young age. The illumination of this knowledge awakens the dragon within their soul who moves to investigate the source of the disturbance. Bringing with it the First Change when the dragon momentarily takes over.<br /> <br /> <br /> For European breeds, the First Change is provoked by intense emotion, similar to other Weres who undergo their first change. But it is mostly focused on witnessing injustice and the culprits being allowed to get away with it one too many times. The Noble Wyrm screams from within, demanding retribution be carried out, and their conscience is only too happy to oblige. The First Change may come in response to vigilante justice, as the dragon fulfills the role that society incompetently refuses.<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change for Noble Wyrms is unpredictable in when it can occur. Sometimes it's as early as childhood, other times as late as old age. When it does happen, however, the extended lifespan of the Noble Wyrm is activated. Leading to stories of men and women discovering the secret to immortality, and children who possess wisdom far beyond their years. In ancient times, such children were even known to ascend to the throne, becoming child emperors and kings before such systems were abolished in modern society.<br /> <br /> <br /> ----------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Available Breeds:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Qilin</i> - The hooved dragons of Chinese mythology. Those who can look upon a Qilin's true form and not go mad are said to become wise scholars, or illustrious rulers beloved by their people. Known as &quot;Kirin&quot; in the regions of Japan, they are sometimes called the Unicorns of China.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Celestial </i>- Sky serpents of Asian mythology, who are said to be able to control the weather. They possess the gift of magical flight, and their blessings grant others strength, power, or good luck. Their anger brings about natural disasters upon those who displease them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Draco Splenden</i> - Water dragons attributed to tales of giant sea serpents. They are the most beautiful of the European breeds. Although they can not actually breathe underwater, they do have the capacity to stay submerged for several hours before needing to surface for air!<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Draco Rex Cristatus</i> - Earth dragons who lack the iconic ability to fly. They are the largest and strongest of the European breeds. During their life times, they acquire great knowledge, and pass that knowledge onto students they deem worthy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Draco Flameus</i> - Fire dragons who's wrath brings destruction upon the lands of Men. They are the most violent of the European breeds, and also the rarest. Possibly because humans kept sending knights to hunt them down!<br /> <br /> <br /> ------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Forms:</b> Unlike most breeds, Noble Wyrms possess only two forms. The human form, and a Dire Beast form.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Human</i> - The human form of a Noble Wyrm has one of two possible appearances. Either one of great wealth, prestige, and beauty, or one of great poverty, scorn, and hideousness. The later form is used to test the hearts of others and discern if they are worthy of the gifts the dragon can offer. The prior form is used to inspire, advise, or lead society towards a better path.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Dire Beast</i> - The true form of the Noble Wyrm is an enormous reptile larger than almost any land animal. They can stand as tall as a house when on all fours, and measure between 20 to 40 feet long from nose to tail. The serpentine dragons such the Celestials and the Draco Splendens are the longest species, while the Qilin are the smallest. When in this form, Draco Flameus gain wings and the ability to fly, while Draco Splendens gain fins and enhanced swimming capabilities. The Dire Beast form is naturally quadrupedal, but the European breeds can stand on their hind legs in exchange for a loss in m</span>ovement speed.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Noble Wyrms are creatures of justice and wisdom, so when they enter a rage state, they do so without remorse or hesitation. When enraged, a Noble Wyrm taps into the pain and suffering of innocents who have yet to be compensated for their losses. They also tap into the remorse and shame of those for who the opportunity for learning has been lost to them, or the act of knowledge being turned to destructive applications. The indignity of a crime gone unpunished, or the shame of clinging to ignorance fuels the Dragon's wrath, and they turn it upon those who are guilty of depriving the world of such. When a Dragon is enraged, they do not stop at the ring leader of the crime, they seek to punish all who had a part in it.<br /> <br /> In times past when the Draco Flameus were more numerous and society more ignorant, their rage caused them to burn down entire countrysides and gorge themselves upon armies of soldiers until they felt they had purged everyone responsible for the deed, or until a Hunter was sent to slay them. Since these ancient times, Noble Wyrms have saw fit to keep their rage more narrowly focused, but it is no less terrifying than it was thousands of years ago.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-29-2014, 11:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-29T08:06:49-08:00","id":325286,"title":"","content":"How'd I do? ^_^","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-29T08:43:50-08:00","id":325310,"title":"","content":"It seems nice!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-29T15:33:56-08:00","id":325618,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\">Thanks, I was hoping I did good. I couldn't think of tribal names, so I grabbed an idea commonly attributed to western Dragons: Latin!<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Qilin Form:</b> The smallest of the Noble Wyrms also has the most exotic looking animal shape. The Qilin is depicted as having the hooves of a horse, the head of a tiger, and draconic scales covering its body. It also has a pair of small antlers regardless of gender.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Celestial Form:</b> The iconic serpents of Asian mythology. Celestial Dragons, as they are popularly known, have a snake-like body, with short legs ending in 3 toed claws. While in this form, a magical organ located in the brain gives them the power to fly, enabling them to soar above the clouds and remain out of sight of the mortals below. These dragons tend to have a pair of blunted antlers and whisker mustaches are common as well.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Splenden Form:</b> The giant sea serpents of European folk lore. Draco Splendens have no arms or legs, rendering it impossible for them to use human tools while in their animal form. They do, however, possess tremendous body strength, able to snap ships (excluding military vessels) in half with their coils. Although this form can slither about on land and breathes air, it has evolved for life in the water and is not as agile when removed from it's preferred environment. This form has fins along it's tail, head, and body which make for better steering and propulsion in the water.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rex Cristatus Form:</b> The towering lizards of European folk lore. Draco Rex Cristatus are the largest of the non-serpent forms. A giant lizard that can weigh several tons and smash through rock like it was wood. Their scales make them tough as tanks, but they're about as slow as one too. What they gain in raw power, they lose in mobility.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Flameus Form:</b> The iconic flying lizards of European folk lore. Draco Flameus manifest a set of leathery wings that can support them in the skies. But a giant, flying lizard is rather conspicuous in densely populated areas. In order to gain the ability to fly, Flameus are smaller and lighter than Rex Cristatus, and become much more agile than their ground dwelling counter parts.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T17:22:01-08:00","id":348650,"title":"","content":"Just a thought. I've seen a lot of dog shifter ideas, but I had an interest spin on things. For now, let's call them the Witch Hounds, descended, they claim, from children of Hecate. Strange, fey creatures who bind and enslave spirits to serve them or their allies, who know a massive number of Rituals lost to the Uratha, and view the children of Father Wolf as dangerous pests or useful customers. Just an idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-24T11:52:19-08:00","id":380321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=149429#post149429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I just went and did some research. Kappa apparently are known for stealing a person's <i>shirikodama. </i>A mythical ball said to contain a person's soul..<br /> <br /> It's located in the anus!<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Maybe they use Shirikodamas for create more Kappas or rituals ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/343-suleri-drals","name":"Suleri Drals","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-25T01:24:05-08:00","id":380856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=348650#post348650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a thought. I've seen a lot of dog shifter ideas, but I had an interest spin on things. For now, let's call them the Witch Hounds, descended, they claim, from children of Hecate. Strange, fey creatures who bind and enslave spirits to serve them or their allies, who know a massive number of Rituals lost to the Uratha, and view the children of Father Wolf as dangerous pests or useful customers. Just an idea.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Well we all know wolves came before dogs, so I think the rituals and the old ways would be more accessible to them, than shifters who were essentially domesticated. Which was the premise behind my dog-shifter idea.<br /> <br /> <br /> As for Kappa using it in rituals, I can understand that. But how would you role play obtaining it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-25T08:10:03-08:00","id":381017,"title":"","content":"Only <i>sort of</i> shifters, but I plan to use Lamprey Hosts in my next chronicle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-25T19:09:08-08:00","id":381507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Acrozatarim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=381017#post381017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Only <i>sort of</i> shifters, but I plan to use Lamprey Hosts in my next chronicle.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Gross, a person exploding into lampreys! On the bright side though, they can't escape if they're on land.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-26T07:46:02-08:00","id":381968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=381507#post381507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Gross, a person exploding into lampreys! On the bright side though, they can't escape if they're on land.</div> </div> </div> </div>They'll probably mostly hang out in damp areas where they can slither away into the murk, and near rivers and so forth.<br /> <br /> I figure they'll use the Gauntlet to latch onto prey from and drink of them, vampire-style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-27T13:41:28-07:00","id":994194,"title":"","content":"Sorry to necro this, but I wanted to bring this topic back up without making another thread, especially since we have reason to be hopeful. We've seen Skinchangers and WatP-style Shifters in Dark Eras, and I've seen a lot of interest from fans and OP writers in the topic. Now, I don't want to get ahead of myself or make predictions or say anything's coming, it's just exciting to see that there's so much interest. <br /> <br /> The one Skinchanger idea that I had that I'm really proud of was dinosaur-skinchangers, who need authentic dinosaur bone instead of hide. Their secret is that they don't actually turn into dinosaurs- they gather reptile-spirits and fear-spirits and power-spirits and turn into a big scaly monster that's more of a physical manifestation of the pop culture image of dinosaurs. <br /> <br /> I also want to do some hereditary shifter types and messed-up families, because that's something I liked about Leviathan that ChroD doesn't have much of. I understand wanting to avoid genetic or hereditary splats, but there's a lot of potential for family curses and conspiracies and that sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-28T06:40:38-07:00","id":994536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Geckopirateship</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=994194#post994194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The one Skinchanger idea that I had that I'm really proud of was <b>dinosaur-skinchangers</b>, who need authentic dinosaur bone instead of hide. Their secret is that they don't actually turn into dinosaurs- <b>they gather reptile-spirits and fear-spirits and power-spirits and turn into a big scaly monster that's more of a physical manifestation of the pop culture image of dinosaurs.</b></div> </div> </div> </div><i>Please, go on.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-28T12:25:28-07:00","id":994749,"title":"","content":"I didn't really get any farther than that or into any gameplay mechanics, but I did get the general concept that they weren't turning into actual dinosaurs, they were turning into the abstract ideal of the dinosaur as it appears in pop culture. So they'd definitely have some sort of fear-inducing power, because of the &quot;oh shit oh shit oh fuck oh fuck it's a dinosaur&quot; mental effect that dinosaurs have. Dragons don't make people shit themselves the way the T-Rex from Jurassic Park did. No matter how much paleontologists discover, it'll be hard to dislodge Rex from the space it embodies in the collective unconscious, where it's less of an animal and more a living symbol of destruction and power and predation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T14:01:16-07:00","id":995335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Geckopirateship</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=994749#post994749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> where it's less of an animal and more a living symbol of destruction and power and predation.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Actually, research has begun to show signs that the T-Rex may have been a scavenger. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T14:02:32-07:00","id":995338,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=995335#post995335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Actually, research has begun to show signs that the T-Rex may have been a scavenger. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>That is literally exactly 100% the point I was making. The cultural image of T-Rex has nothing to do with current science, and everything to do with the human need to create monsters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T17:20:31-07:00","id":995443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Geckopirateship</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=994194#post994194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I also want to do some hereditary shifter types and messed-up families, because that's something I liked about Leviathan that ChroD doesn't have much of. I understand wanting to avoid genetic or hereditary splats, but there's a lot of potential for family curses and conspiracies and that sort of thing.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Ironic since werewolves are initially portrayed as something you have to be born as, instead of turned into one via a bite or something. But I've been told later editions of the game changed that premise, somewhat.<br /> <br /> I also came up with my own version of a way to ritually infuse a human with shifter essence through a bite, thus turning them into such creatures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-29-2016, 08:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T17:23:41-07:00","id":995446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=995443#post995443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ironic since werewolves are initially portrayed as something you have to be born as, instead of turned into one via a bite or something. But I've been told later editions of the game changed that premise, somewhat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, they can now be pop up at random, or be changed through encounters with werewolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T17:27:12-07:00","id":995448,"title":"","content":"I forget, do the Pure still have their weirdo obsession with purity and breeding? I like them having that as a contrast to the Forsaken. <br /> <br /> Messed-up supernatural families are good antagonists for &quot;creepy town in the middle of nowhere&quot; plots, because you can have an isolated town or area that's completely controlled by one family. The Oberlochs from Bloodlines: The Hidden and the Coalblacks from Skinchangers were what I was thinking of, here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T18:06:23-07:00","id":995459,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Geckopirateship</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/136859-what-s-your-custom-shifters?p=995448#post995448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I forget, do the Pure still have their weirdo obsession with purity and breeding? I like them having that as a contrast to the Forsaken.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on what you mean by purity. They mean it as in pure of Luna's influence. Breeding, not so much, especially since werewolves can now reproduce with other werewolves.<br /> <br /> The Ivory Claws are probably the most likely to be considered as having an obsession with breeding, as they 'believe that Urfarah\u2019s heritage is passed on in the blood, and through purity they may rebuild Pangaea.' So I would expect them to keep it mostly within werewolves. The examples show that they are aware of the consequences of in-breeding and occasionally seek out outside wolf-blooded and worthy humans for marriage and mating.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Messed-up supernatural families are good antagonists for &quot;creepy town in the middle of nowhere&quot; plots, because you can have an isolated town or area that's completely controlled by one family. The Oberlochs from Bloodlines: The Hidden and the Coalblacks from Skinchangers were what I was thinking of, here. </div> </div> </div>The Pure have a few of those. The Rios in Mexico, Keeltown, Colorado, Los Asesinos. But it's hardly something that would be confined to the Pure. The Forsaken have the Pickerings and Bloody Mary's family. A family is an easy foundation for a pack, and with their abilities a small town could easily become dominated by one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-29-2016, 09:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-30T12:41:49-07:00","id":995754,"title":"","content":"I actually had a weredragons thing I made back when we were doing a 1e site. I mean, I know there is a weredragons on this list, but this is... something of a different beast. I will probably convert it to 2e if they make a 2e version of watP - though I could probably figure out a method to do it anyways even if they don't. *considers* Anyways. It's a little edited from it's original form because the old one didn't work in all chronicles but... well, here it is. <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\">The Mirska</span><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Mythology</span></u></b><br /> <br /> <i>In the beginning, the world was covered in darkness. Nothing but thick, black primordial shadow - a shadow that was vivid and alive, consuming the fabric of all that was. In this darkness the earth was nothing but dirt and wind, a cold and bleak desert hell which bore no heat and no vision. In this darkness existed only alien creatures that had existed since the beginning of time, born from the darkness and seeking only to dominate and rule over it - perhaps to even overcome it. Some spirits remember this reality as Pangaea, or the World~That~Was~Whole, but the Dragons only know it as the World~That~Was.<br /> <br /> Within the darkness was born the stars - and among those stars rose to power Seven Great beings, but only one of them important to this story: Helios, the Sun and the Ruler of the World by light. Helios saw the earth and the Darkness, and saw the creatures within it - monstrosities and terrors and horrifying visions that knew nothing but eternal destruction. Like all before him, he sought dominion over this world, but unlike many he struck with a fierce rage and an unbearable strength.<br /> <br /> He banished the Darkness itself from his presence. From him was birthed his Army, who even now fight within the Deep Shadow eternally. Unyielding and unforgiving, he cast a deathly gaze to the earth. But though his wrath was terrible, his Dominion was just: Beneath his gaze, new life was allowed to prosper. Life that was not destructive to the Whole. He sought to encase the world in his light, and destroy all those who fell beneath him. And when this era came, is when the Dragon's speak of Pangaea's Birth as the World~That~Was~Whole.<br /> <br /> But those condemned to the Darkness were not so satisfied. They saw the Sun's Dominion, and wanted it for themselves. They saw the Sun's cruelty to them, and wanted to destroy them. They plotted amongst themselves, great and terrible beings that the mind could not fathom. They argued and quarrelled, but eventually agreed that Helios must be destroyed, and that they must work together to destroy them. They agreed between them a champion that world represent the Darkness, and bestowed upon that champion powers that no being could ever have. And within that champion, they bestilled within them all the things best in the dark. <br /> <br /> That champion's name was Zirnitra - A Dragon God of Sorcery. Zirnitra ensorcelled itself into the form of a female being, who stood at the edge of Helios' Domain. There, she fought against the entities of the Darkness, to prove to the world her ferocity, her cunning, and her power. As Helios' Watched this being, he began to fall for her - a warrior as he was, and a champion at that. He came to her, and they consummated their love. And within her embrace, Zirnitra took it's true form, and swallowed the Sun whole.<br /> <br /> And the world was encased within Darkness, with a new ruler of Helios' Domain. And it could never be argued, by man or beast, that it was the Dragon who had defeated the Sun, and who swallowed it whole. Because of his spot within the Dragon's belly, the beast was bestowed with Helios' fire, which granted it a weapon against those who would challenge it's domain. And under it's rule, life wilted.<br /> <br /> It would come a time that Zirnitra would then seek to build her army. She started simply, with a set of eggs born of her from the consummation with the sun. She kept the eggs hot with her breath, and close as a mother would.<br /> <br /> The eggs hatched, and when they hatched, their minds were opened by the light of their father, with the memories of both mother and father. They saw their parent's cruelty, but knew of the result on either path - on one, life and on another, destruction. And no sooner had the eggs hatched than they set upon their mother, tearing her limb from limb and devouring her piece by piece.<br /> <br /> As as they devoured her, they set free the Sun. As he looked down, he saw what had become of them, and they looked to him as they looked to their mother, and spoke to their father upon the destroyed corpse of their mother.<br /> <br /> &quot;Look,&quot; They cried to their father, high in the sky, &quot;And know this, Sun God. For though you walk this world now, your path is one of life. We accept this path, and we choose it. However...&quot; The Eldest crushed a bone beneath his foot, and snarled, &quot;The Darkness is as much a part of the world as the light. Should you enshroud it fully, you would be no different than that of our mother, and you would destroy this world in your fire. If you walk this path, if you seek to destroy this world, Father, then we will grant you no mercy. Beneath our teeth and fangs, we will rend you as we have our mother, if it need be that we take the place of the sun itself.&quot;<br /> <br /> And at this outrage, the Sun roared - in Anger, but also in Respect, for he saw within them the light of himself, and the darkness of their mother. He agreed to keep his light to only one half of the world, and to share it with another, but in return the Dragon's world serve him - serve to ensure that never again did the world become encased in the darkness of the World~That~Was.</i><br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:18px\">World-View</span></b></u><br /> <br /> The Dragons are real. They've been there, ever since the beginning and a bit before then - in a land before time and in a world that the world does not remember. But the Mirska remember - they remember everything. Born with the genetic code of all individuals before them, they have samples of the memories of past lives few creatures are privy to, and it's this unique ability that allowed them to know of their mothers crimes and make an appropriate judgement.<br /> <br /> The Dragons task themselves not with the protection of man or monster, but of the protection of what they call 'the balance'. They recognize two primordial forces - light and darkness, and seek to ensure that neither gain the upper hand over the other, because to do such would destroy the world and allow the advent of the Things~Before. The reality is, this creed is left very loose because though the dragons remember, these memories are kept in bits and pieces, making little sense the further back it returns. <br /> <br /> Humanity still remembers the dragons in the height of their prime. They remember a group of Wise Gods, the source of all magic and the teachers of man. The remember beasts which ruled the wilds, and who taught man all it knew of the secrets of the other world. They remember a dying breed, which was both beautiful, glorious, and terrifying. They taught humanity much -- far too much.<br /> <br /> The Dragons are responsible for many of the occultic secrets that the world is aware of today or so they claim. How to hide from the creatures of the dark and how to use the light to it's advantage. It was their words that rose the Sun to such predominance, and their power which was given to humanity to defend themselves. But humanity was a creature that feared all that was more powerful in it, and the dragons were arrogant.<br /> <br /> Their fall arose around the period of time that many individuals call the dark ages - and it would have ended there, had it not been for the choice actions of a few brave Dragons, who sacrificed themselves for the rest of their kind. Driven and hunted through the machinations of the creatures of the Dark, the Dragons numbered as few as a hundred before they actually realized they had lost the earth and failed in their duties. And it was during this time that a small clutch of dragons made a sacrifice, offering their lives to a powerful incantation that would forever afflict the world - the extinction of their reptilian forms, for the similar extinction of the true memory of their existence, for a time until the dragons were needed.<br /> <br /> The power worked, afflicting the Dragons with much smaller, more innocuous forms that would never be seen as dangerous by the mortals - but in exchange, they fell into rumor, hidden creatures hiding within their burrowed holes, waiting until the time would come where they returned. As the power afflicted them, they too began to forget - to forget what they were and their power. Individuals who held the blessing of their mother and father became not the beasts of old, but men and women described as mad, sun-touched or otherwise outcasted.<br /> <br /> And then, in the early 1940s, something began to change. A great wave enveloped the world as a creature of violence and corruption stepped out from it's own realm and into the Shadow. The results were so horrific that they still haunt the world to this day. With this creature stepping forward, the chains of the spell cast so long ago upon the Dragons began to falter and break . The souls of the dragons which nestled in the hearts of men of mad dreams began to rise, and they began to remember the past - both that of those who had suffered during their slumber, and that of before, of the histories and stories they knew and their rise to power.<br /> <br /> By the middle of the decade, the Dragons had fully roused and made their efforts to return the creature from whence it came. When the dust finally cleared, the creature was gone and the Dragons made aware of a world that was vastly different from the one which they slept in - but the full picture they held before the slumbering had not returned.They remembered shattered bits and pieces of thousands if not millions of individual lifetimes, and seeking to understand the ones which existed during the slumbering has driven many mad. With the creature gone, many are cursed with the memory of a purpose, but not the recollection of how they fulfilled that purpose. Some question that the Slumbering ought to have recalled them after the event, and wonder if the creature which stepped into the Hisil during that time was merely a herald of something else.<br /> <br /> Something worse.<br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Systems</span></b></u><br /> <br /> Inborn Aspects: Shapeshifting, Regeneration, Breath-Attack<br /> <br /> Custom Aspect: Breath Attack - Children of the Dragon who swallowed the sun, the Mirska still hold a shard of him within their chest. Because of this, the Mirska all benefit from the ability to breath fire. The Mirska spends an essence point, and rolls Dexterity + Athletics + Primal Urge, Penalized by Armor and Defense as appropriate. The Range is equal to a number of yards equal to 20 x Primal Urge. Any target successfully hit with this attack suffers lethal damage each turn equal to the Mirska's primal urge rating until the fire is put out. A Dragon's Breath is not a normal fire - it comes directly from Helios. As a Result, any creature who would be harmed by sunlight or fire is especially vulnerable to this, carrying the qualities of both.<br /> <br /> Weakness: Unlike most shifters, the Dragons are not actually allergic to the silver metal, and can use it and wield it without issue. That said, they are actually weak to another precious metal - gold. Helios remembers his time in Zirnitra's belly, and though the dragons are seen as his servants, that fear still echoes within him - as such, gold, which holds a strong metaphysical connection to him, seems to resonate with that spiritual fear and serves as a weapon against the Mirska. It is perhaps odd then that the dragons hold such a love of gold, to the point that many wealthy or powerful dragons will adorn themselves with the metal or hoarde it within caves- but then again, avoiding it seems as though it would clue people in, doesn't it?<br /> <br /> Reproduction: The Dragons, like their lizard forms, seem to have a great difficulty reproducing - at least, reproducing weredragons. While the dragons themselves bear no mortal inclination against mating, it will almost always uniformly result in a Dragon Blooded. If one were to go by their lizard cousins, it would be assumed that birthing an actual Were-Dragon could only be performed at a specific time, every two years - and only in the natural wilds rather than in the mortal society, where the spiritual connection is thinnest.<br /> <br /> Still, the dragons exist despite these harsh restrictions - during the Slumber, Blooded and Slumbering Dragons alike felt the natural inclination to perform this ritual, where they would begin to migrate towards climates what fit appropriately to the dragons needs and lie together in estatic splendor. Many then would attribute this act as a loss of senses, a madness creeping in, but even then those who concieved true dragons were rare until the Awakening, as in order to become a dragon you not only had to follow this ritual, but had to concieve within nature - under the light of the sun.<br /> <br /> There are two types of weredragons - The most common type today are those who are born a Mirska, notably called &quot;Pure Bloods&quot;. These shifters are the ones who followed the above method to being born. When a Weredragon is born, they are born with a caul over their head - a thick layer of scaled, fleshy skin that is easily peeled off to reveal the child beneath. From birth, the child is capable of shifting into it's infant animal form, though much like walking it needs to learn these things in time -- and during the Slumber, shifting was all but impossible.<br /> <br /> The other type are notable more rare though it is believed it will gain more commonality as the Dragons return. The process is through a ritual, crafted of Dragon Alchemy, in which a Dragon, after consuming the potion, consumates with another shifter to create a Dragon's Egg. The Dragon's Egg is a spiritual phenomena that is solely accessable by the Mirska. The egg may be implanted within the heart of a Blooded mortal - generally of a type associated with the one consummated with. The process is not gentle and very risky - while the blooded is alive, the dragon must tear out the mortal's heart cleanly, with their own claws, and replace the heart with the egg before the mortal dies. If successful, the egg begins to beat in place of the heart, and will hatch within a month. The process will evoke a phenomena similar to the first change - traumatic, bloody, and often related to the auspice in which the dragon will be created.<br /> <br /> It should be noted that, while a target does not need to be willing to undergo this process, that it is both traumatic and painful and the target is under no compulsion to assist the Dragon that turned them. In fact, arrogance may blind a dragon so that he believes his target will take this blessing well, only to find the new dragon seeking every avenue to destroy their spiritual parent.<br /> <br /> <b>Human</b>: The mortal form of a dragon typically tends to build muscle in a lean, athletic way rather than a bulky stocky way. They typically have an almost golden texture to their skin, and many might (after realizing their nature) describe their features as serpentine or reptilian. Perhaps most notably of all, however, is that all Dragons in their mortal form bear a birthmark, a tell of sorts which is a symbol of their status.<br /> Modifiers: +2 to perception rolls. Attempts to identify them as supernatural beings suffer the standard -2 penalty.<br /> <br /> <b>Near-Man</b>: Taking on the dragonic form while maintaining a sense of what it is to be human, this form is typically used as a sort of battle form for the dragon, especially for a trained or militant one. Their arms develop gauntlet like scales, as do their legs - their skin takes on a soft reptilian pallor, and their eyes always turn a shade golden. If the Dragon has the Wings or Additional Limbs Aspect (for a tail) these features can optionally be accessed in this form, giving the dragon an almost demon-like appearance.<br /> Modifiers: +2 to Strength, + 1 to Stamina, +1 Size, +2 Health, +2 Speed, +2 Perception. Bite for 0L or Claw for 2L. All Strength rolls, including attacks, gain the 9 again benefit. Use the higher of Dexterity or Wits to calculate Defense. <br /> <br /> <b>War-Form</b>: Combining the form of the Sungazer lizard and human, the Mirska grows a full two to three feet taller and puts on roughly two hundred pounds of weight. The legs and body become scaled and jagged, and the head takes on a lizard's face. The eyes glow with a golden light, and the inside of the Mirska's mouth glows with the threat of fire to erupt. If the Dragon has the Wings or Additional Limbs Aspect (for a tail), these aspects are added to this form.<br /> Modifiers: +4 Strength, +2 Stamina, +2 Size, +4 Health, +4 Speed, +3 Perception, Bite for 3L or Claw for 2L, bulletproof armor equal to half their Primal Urge score. All Strength-based rolls, including attacks, gain the 8-again quality. Use the higher of Dexterity or Wits to calculate Defense.<br /> <br /> <b>Dire-Beast</b>: The often forgotten form of the original dragon, this creature seems to be a much larger version of the animal form in which the Dragons are born to. Standing on four legs at the size of the average man, and weighing between two to three hundred pounds, it's hard to believe this reptile can fly if it has wings - never the less, if they hold the aspect the option is readily available to them. In this form, the creature's eyes take on a golden hue, and their chest seems to glow with a golden light, especially when they are about to breath fire upon the enemy.<br /> Modifiers: Strength +2, Dexterity +2, Stamina +2, +1 Size, +3 Health, +7 Speed, +2 Perception, Bite for 2L or Claw for 1L. All Strength-based rolls, including attacks, gain the 9-again quality. Use the higher of Dexterity or Wits to calculate Defense.<br /> <br /> <b>Animal-Form</b>: Tiny and adorable, the Dragons claim that this was not their original form - merely the end result of a sacrificed ritual designed to protect them all from death's gaze. By default, a Dragon's animal form mimicks that of the armadillo girdled lizard. A character who takes on other aspects such as camouflage may appear as a chameleon, or one with wings may take on the form of the Draco Volans - but it always seems to take on a small lizards form.<br /> Modifiers: Dexterity +2, Stamina +1, Size 1, Health -3, Speed +5, Perception +2, Bite for 0L. Use the higher of Dexterity or Wits to Calculate Defense.<br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> Auspice: Like werewolves, the Mirska benefit from Auspices of their own, which denotes their spiritual position within the Draconic Society. This Auspice is typically decided by the time of day in which the Mirska was born - or in the case of the Turned, the time of day of their first change. Because it is not possible to birth a dragon outside of the light of Helios, there are no (known) dragons who do not have an auspice or have a night related auspice... or at least, that is what the Dragon's believe. Ancient memories suggest another truth of darker things which corrupt dragons within the womb, allowing for them to be born in the night... but such things are hearsay and rumor, nothing more. <br /> <br /> <b>The Farseers</b>- Born at Dawn, the Farseers are the innovators of the future. It's said that it is the Farseers who crafted Dragon Alchemy, but whether or not that is true stands to debate, as Mnesis does not actually seem to provide the answer of the dragon's nature. Born with the ability to see the upcoming day, the Farseers benefit from prophetic dreams: Once per story, the player may ask the Storyteller for a dream of prophecy, providing some clue about the challenges facing the Farseer. The Farseer must sleep for at least four hours in order to dream of the future. The dream is always veiled in symbolism that the character must interpret. In addition, the Farseer automatically gains one die to any Occult rolls made to interpret omens or to solve occult riddles. Associated with the Dawn Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Burning Ones </b>- Born of the Rising Sun, the Burning Ones are the Warrior Dragons. Known for their Ferocity, the Burning Ones will tear asunder any who stand in their way or threaten what they call theirs. They are blessed with the Burning Talon's Benefit: The burning ones claws burn hot, to the point that they may add their Primal Urge Penalty to any attack roll. Furthermore, this additional damage is treated as heat damage (if bashing) or fire damage (if lethal), afflicting supernaturals who would call these things their banes respectively. Associated with the Searings Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Sun Priests </b>- Born when the sun is at it's highest peak. The Chosen Sorcerers of the Dragon's, the Sun Priests dedicate their time to the study of the supernatural, the hidden and the known. They benefit from the Ancient Foe's memory - any time engaging with a supernatural phenomena, the Sun Priests may roll their Mnesis rating to gather 'information' about the entity relevant to them. Associated with the Firelings Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Warm Ones</b> - Born during the Setting sun, when the sun begins to cool and gentle against earths creatures. Known as Healers, for their part, the Warm Ones are blessed with the Healer's Eye: With a reflexive Wits + Composure roll, the Warm One can identify the wounds and sicknesses which ail an individual. This typically provides the Warm One with a bonus equal to the successes rolled on all rolls to 'heal' an afflicted individual. Associated with the Warmlings Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Lore Keepers</b> - Born at Dusk, the Lore Keepers, as their name implies, are tasked with the memory and history of the Mirska - they bear a unique and powerful connection to the memories that the Dragons gather, to such an extent that they can tap into the memories at will. The Lore Keepers may buy Mnesis at half cost. Further, once per scene they may choose to roll their Mnesis rating to tap into their ancient memory. Each success may be used to increase a skill of their choice, as the Lore Keeper taps into past lives and makes them their own. Associated with the Dusk Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Hidden Ones </b>- Born on a day so cloudy that it hides even the Sun, the Hidden Ones are those dragons whom have been blessed with the ability to hide even in the light. They benefit from the Chameleon's Curse - Any rolls to notice these individuals are penalized by the Dragon's Primal Urge Penalty. Oddly enough, they are associated with the Illuminator's Descant.<br /> <br /> <b>The Crowned Ones</b> - Born on a day which the Sun is Eclipsed, these Dragons are beyond the concept of rare - they are practically unheard of. Still, memories remember the Crowned Ones with a fervent fondness as the Princes of the Dragons, those whose ability granted them the Lordly Tongue, Which allows both communication and command to lesser entities, typically lesser animals and entities.<br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> <b>Rage:</b> Ferocious and full of Avarice, the Dragon that loses himself to rage is a terrifying thing to behold. When the rage strikes, it fills the Mirska with memories of the Slumber - of all the Slumbering Dragons who were called mad for their dreams, their memories of distant times, and all the persecution and destruction reigned on the Dragon's brethren. He loses himself to an unstoppable killing frenzy, not able to tell the difference between friend or foe. Once all has been destroyed in his path, the Mirska is haunted by the memories and images which filled it for the next twenty four hours - they have difficulty telling which memories are theirs or their predecessors, and may develop the fear that they will be hunted again and tortured for their 'madness'. (Paranoia).<br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>~New Merit: Mnesis (1-5)~</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <i>Note: Based on Ancient Echoes from banishers.</i><br /> <br /> Prerequisites: Mirska or Mirska Blooded 4<br /> <br /> Effect: The Mirska retain the genetic memory of those who came before them - like past lives, they remember the lives of their mother and father, and their great grand father - on and on until the beginning of time. These memories are fractured, broken, and maddening - but some try to make sense of it. This merit reflects the memories a Dragon can make sense of. Each dot in this merit also adds to any rolls related to remembering an event, or to any roll which can be supplimented by the Mirska's ancient memory.<br /> <br /> (1) Deja vu The character has clear memories of experiences a in a past life up to 200 years ago, i.e., something that happened during the Victorian era (19th century) or later (20th century). The character may receive simply a feeling (&quot;I feel as if I've walked down this street before when it was cobblestones instead of asphalt.&quot;) or a more profound &quot;memory.&quot; (&quot;I followed a man into this library and through this hallway once before -- long ago. There was a locked door and then the restricted books section.&quot;).<br /> <br /> (2) Memory's Thin Walls The character's memory or seeming memory extends back as far as 400 years (the 17th century). The more recent the memory, the more vivid its details are to the dragon. (&quot;I remember watching fascinated as a child while some of my uncles shoved heavy crates of tea into the waters of the harbor.&quot;) or (&quot;I remember the stars were in the same position in the sky when a man darted across my path fleeing something indescribable and unspeakable -- much like that!&quot;)<br /> <br /> (3) Memory's Open Door The character can remember something (or something similar) having occurred as far back as the 13th century. (&quot;I've seen the signs before in this very house -- a plague house!&quot;) Dragons can sometimes experience premonitions based on past events at this level of the Merit. (&quot;The earth shook here once before, and I feel it growing restless again.&quot;)<br /> <br /> (4) Stepping Backward The character's memory can extend as far back as the beginning of the Common Era (1st century) and the amount of detail remembered may seem to place the character back in time, though this is certainly not an instance of time travel. (&quot;We hunted even then, when Man was still building their mud huts and their straw roofs, we hoarded all that was valuable, secrets lost and hidden.)<br /> <br /> (5) Echoes of the World~That~Came~Before: At the highest level of this Merit, the character seems to remember aspects of Pangaea itself. Dragons learn ancient secrets lost to the throes of humanity, of relics and disasters seeking to come and return. An ancient beast comes, and they remember it, and where it was born. Dragons of this level sometimes recieve insights into the ways of ancient and lost spirits. They might experience this level with hallucinatory-like intensity or as a waking nightare; the memories of that time are the memories of symbolism rather than the literal thoughts and perceptions Dragons are accustomed to now, which makes gathering information from them difficult and frustrating.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">~New Aspect: Dragon Alchemy~</span></b></u><br /> <br /> The blood of a dragon is a potent mystical source. The blood of a slain dragon is depicted as either beneficent or as poisonous in medieval legend and literary fiction. In German legend, dragon blood has the power to render invincible skin or armor bathed in it, as is the case with Siegfried's skin or Ortnit's armor. In the Slavic myth, the Earth refuses it as it is so vile that Mother Earth wishes not to have it within her womb, and it remains above ground for all eternity. The blood of the dragon in Beowulf has acidic qualities, allowing it to seep through iron. Heinrich von Winkelried dies after the blood of the dragon slain by him accidentally drips on him.<br /> <br /> And as always, there is always a hint of truth to the story. The Dragons have learned, throughout the years, to tap into the divinity within her blood and use it to their advantage. Through the study of their blood in relationship to alchemy they managed to create a set of mystical practices in their own form of ritual. Dragon Alchemy is an Aspect bought at new dots x7. Each dot purchased grants one Alchemical Ritual of the equivalent level to the Dragon, and allows them to purchase future rituals at a cost of ritual dots x 2. Each concoction created through the use of Dragon Alchemy requires a list of materials to meld into the dragons blood, and of couse, the blood cost itself. In game terms, each potion requires the dragon to take a number of lethal equal to the level of the ritual created.<br /> <br /> As a curious note, it is actually possible for Dragon Blooded to learn this art - unfortunately the thinness of the Dragon's Blood coursing through the Dragon Blooded makes the cost significantly higher for the blooded, to the point that those with weak blood would die before being able to use the most potent of these rituals. If a Dragon Blooded's blood is used for this, the cost is a number of lethal equal to (7 - Blooded Merit) per ritual dot. <br /> <br /> ~Theorized Rites~<br /> Level 1<br /> <br /> Armor of the Elements<br /> Sense Twilight Entities<br /> <br /> Salamander's Oil - Despite all of their strengths, Mirska do not actually have any protection against fire - Helios is a sun god, and while his light creates fire, it is not an actual fire god. Alchemists however, say to hell with that, and spill their blood with various chemicals designed as a deterrent of fire, and bath in the resulting oil. As long as an individual is covered in this oil, they are not harmed by fire or heat. If the fire is supernatural in origin, this power grants armor against it equal to the Dragon's Primal Urge.<br /> <br /> Blood Vial - This Chemical is actually fairly base. The Dragon spills their blood, flowing with essence, into a crystal vial which holds the magic within it. The Dragon adds spiritual preservatives, and then corks the vial. When the Dragon - or who ever holds the vial - needs the essence, they simply drink it and add the essence contained into their pool. A Blood Vial can hold a number of essence equal to the Dragon's Primal Urge.<br /> <br /> Level 2<br /> Claws to Twilight<br /> Breath of Poison<br /> Breath of Lightning <br /> <br /> Swallowing the Sky God - Through the swallowing of Helios by Zirnitra, the Dragons gained his light within the fores of their breath. By mixing their blood with various herbs associated with sky and magnetized metal dust, a dragon can create a symbological association with a Sky God, to reproduce this metaphysical transformation. This temporarily costs the dragon their fire breath for a day, but in return they develop an electric breath. When the dragon attacks, he rolls the same roll as before, only now he deals bashing damage. For most living creatures, the lightning will cause the muscles to constrict - for such creatures, remove the 10 again aspect and ones subtract from any physical roll they make for the turn following this attack.<br /> <br /> Vial of True Beauty - Cover yourself in blood, and maintain your youth. While this philosophy is the thought process of psychopaths, when this philosophy is tied to a little diamond dust ground into the blood of a dragon and molded over an individuals flesh, that individual can take on the form of the desired world for a day - in game terms, the Dragon can bestow to someone the benefits of the Striking Looks Merit for a day. <br /> <br /> Level 3<br /> Breath of Petrification<br /> Breath of Frost<br /> <br /> Level 4<br /> Warp a form to become twilight<br /> <br /> Level 5<br /> Dragon Bone - Create a Material which may be forged into a weapon or armor which shifts with you. The Armor may be of any kind, but is similarly difficult to hide the higher it is.<br /> <br /> <b>Common Aspects:</b> Aww!!! (1-5), Burrowing (2-3), Durga's Blessing (2), Unnerving Cry (2), Magnificent (2), Fang and Claw (1-3), Naturl Armor (3-5), Razorskin (3-4), Wings (Limited to Near Man, Hybrid, and Dire-Beast [4]), Hybrid Form (4), War Heart (5 Points)<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T15:30:47-07:00","id":996295,"title":"","content":"I almost can't imagine an occult setting without werejaguar. They're fairly critical to Mayan myth after all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T16:06:48-07:00","id":996862,"title":"","content":"Drawing some ideas from my country's folktales and mythologies...<br /> <br /> Werebears and weretigers claiming descent from figures of mythologies. Daughters of Lady Bear assume mantles of leadership and guardians of the lands beyond, while Sons of the Defiant Tiger claim mountains and deep forests to become its guardian-spirits.<br /> <br /> The infamous werefoxes, wreaking havoc across all of East Asia. They conjure ghost-flames that beguile and seduce men, and work magics of skin-changing. Some of them believe they can transcend their half-bestial states by absorbing human essence and souls.<br /> <br /> Between the above 'big three', I have ideas for snail-wives who devote themselves to one they decide to 'marry', earthworm-men who claim they are the degraded descendents of land-dragons, and snake-monks who mortals lost in the mountains who come to their temples for aid. (The last one is probably some mutated breed of the Snake Host)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-05T17:32:35-08:00","id":1058693,"title":"","content":"<b>Whererabbit:</b> The Prankster<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Wereraccoon:</b> The Thieving<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Weredog:</b> The Guardian<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Werecrow:</b> The Harbinger<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Weretortoise:</b> The Observer<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Weresalmon:</b> The Journey","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-08T06:02:21-08:00","id":1060042,"title":"","content":"Im planning to have some Bison-shifters in my continuos Werewolves of Wyoming - game.<br /> These will be as close to a proper shape shifting Uratha I can get, and (surpirse) they will either be an ally or an enemy of the pack based on their respect of the Bison herds in the county.<br /> <br /> Long story:<br /> The Uratha of Wyoming have banded into one large protectorate in a defensive effort against the blackened wind (an Idigam I made a season ago). Blackened Wind is a forgotten foe of the Great Bison aka &quot;Lord Bison&quot;. Lord Bison is a spirtual ancestor for Bison of Wyoming and also somewhat connected to both the state flag, Yellowstone and Wind River in-game.<br /> In one of the stories our Cahailith found, she discovered a short fragment mentioning the lost &quot;People of the Bison&quot;.<br /> Now, as the Blackened Wind is slowly upon them and they are gathering allies to defend their lands, some spirits and even human allies have mentioned seeing a shifting bison. Player character is a Native American Game Warden, and will be the first to meet one for a very long time. Story has yet to be told, but it is on my backburner. Lots of choices and consequences.<br /> <br /> A the moment it's mostly fluff for me to keep track of in game. Work in progress :-)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/860-ephraim","name":"Ephraim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar860_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}